1
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:05,940
Cool.

2
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:09,160
So thanks so much, everyone, for coming.

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,840
I think you have a pretty cool course here.

4
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:15,680
And thanks, of course, Percy, for inviting me to give a talk.

5
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:19,560
So I think in this class, you're mostly talking about training

6
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:24,960
and how to train these language models, and get to this place

7
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:29,240
where we can have these things that can talk back to.

8
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:31,360
And today, I'm going to talk a little bit

9
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:34,360
about once you have one of those models, what it looks

10
00:00:34,360 --> 00:00:37,440
like from the other side, what it looks like to actually serve

11
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,180
these models, do inference, turn these things from say,

12
00:00:43,180 --> 00:00:46,580
electricity, into tokens, into intelligence.

13
00:00:46,580 --> 00:00:49,640
And also what are some of the fun research problems

14
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:52,200
and research things that you can do when you look

15
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:55,480
at it from that standpoint.

16
00:00:55,480 --> 00:00:59,490
I'll start with some high level motivation.

17
00:00:59,490 --> 00:01:02,170
I think one thing that's become abundantly clear to all of us

18
00:01:02,170 --> 00:01:04,890
is that we are really going through almost

19
00:01:04,890 --> 00:01:06,570
a new industrial revolution in terms

20
00:01:06,570 --> 00:01:09,090
of these models and their capabilities.

21
00:01:09,090 --> 00:01:12,150
These slides I made during my job talks two years ago.

22
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:15,050
So the exact examples are going to be pretty old,

23
00:01:15,050 --> 00:01:19,250
but you can do human-level text generation, code generation,

24
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:26,170
Cursor, Claude Code, GPT 5.5, not just 4.

25
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:28,210
You can generate images and videos

26
00:01:28,210 --> 00:01:31,930
or understand images and videos and process them.

27
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:33,830
You can start to understand new modalities.

28
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:36,090
So we're starting to see some of the applications

29
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:40,390
and the sciences in bio and health, DNA models,

30
00:01:40,390 --> 00:01:42,275
all these sorts of things.

31
00:01:42,275 --> 00:01:43,650
One of the things that has really

32
00:01:43,650 --> 00:01:45,330
been driving my research for a while

33
00:01:45,330 --> 00:01:48,430
is the question of what has made these advances possible,

34
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:52,650
and how can we improve on them in the next generations?

35
00:01:52,650 --> 00:01:56,470
And one abundant driver of these capabilities is really scale.

36
00:01:56,470 --> 00:01:58,890
So again, this is at this point, a

37
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:04,610
pretty old figure but these models have really scaled up

38
00:02:04,610 --> 00:02:05,870
in massive ways.

39
00:02:05,870 --> 00:02:09,729
So in 2018, at the beginning of my PhD in antiquity,

40
00:02:09,729 --> 00:02:12,010
these were hundreds million parameter models

41
00:02:12,010 --> 00:02:12,830
at their largest.

42
00:02:12,830 --> 00:02:15,670
And we were like, oh my God, these things are pretty crazy.

43
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:18,830
By 2019, we thought that they were too dangerous to release,

44
00:02:18,830 --> 00:02:19,970
that's GPT-2.

45
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:23,570
I think in this class, you can train a GPT-2 quality model.

46
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:24,830
I'm not sure if you did.

47
00:02:24,830 --> 00:02:26,390
If you tried hard enough, you could.

48
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,890
And today, of course, you have open source models

49
00:02:31,890 --> 00:02:34,570
that are a trillion parameters and more.

50
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:38,170
Frontier is probably at 5 to 10 trillion parameters.

51
00:02:38,170 --> 00:02:39,730
It's pretty exciting.

52
00:02:39,730 --> 00:02:43,210
With these, you have a bunch of new capabilities like chat,

53
00:02:43,210 --> 00:02:47,170
writing code, analyzing complex text, doing your homework

54
00:02:47,170 --> 00:02:50,410
for you, et cetera.

55
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:55,680
What's really remarkable about this is that this transition is happening faster than we think.

56
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:59,380
So certainly, faster than I thought at the beginning

57
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:00,760
of my PhD a few years ago.

58
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:03,900
So I think one really apt analogy, and the dates

59
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,960
actually match up in an interesting way.

60
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:10,915
So in 1902, there are 130,000 working horses in Manhattan.

61
00:03:10,915 --> 00:03:12,540
So these are horses that you don't just

62
00:03:12,540 --> 00:03:15,020
have around for giggles, but you actually

63
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:19,580
have them because they play some key role.

64
00:03:19,580 --> 00:03:22,700
And they would-- these horses would produce--

65
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:24,820
each would produce pounds of manure a day,

66
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:27,840
times 130,000 horses, you have a real manure problem.

67
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,300
So in fact, there were entire conferences

68
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:32,660
that were just gathered around the question

69
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:34,940
of-- entire academic conferences around,

70
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:37,800
what do we do about all the poop that these horses produce?

71
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:40,712
So in 1898, they actually had one of these in New York.

72
00:03:40,712 --> 00:03:42,420
And their conclusion from that conference

73
00:03:42,420 --> 00:03:44,670
was, there's nothing we can do about the horse manure.

74
00:03:44,670 --> 00:03:47,580
You just have to hold your nose and deal with it.

75
00:03:47,580 --> 00:03:52,660
10 years later, by 1912, cars had already outnumbered horses

76
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:53,530
in Manhattan.

77
00:03:53,530 --> 00:03:56,070
So that 10 year transition, you saw

78
00:03:56,070 --> 00:04:01,790
these things that have been around for centuries

79
00:04:01,790 --> 00:04:05,750
start to be replaced by cars.

80
00:04:05,750 --> 00:04:07,770
And I think for us, for language models,

81
00:04:07,770 --> 00:04:09,990
For a lot of the stuff that we do,

82
00:04:09,990 --> 00:04:12,690
that 1912 moment was probably last year.

83
00:04:12,690 --> 00:04:15,430
So at least for me, last year, I started

84
00:04:15,430 --> 00:04:18,810
writing the majority of my code using these language models.

85
00:04:18,810 --> 00:04:20,589
Most people in my team do it.

86
00:04:20,589 --> 00:04:23,270
I tell all my students to do it, except when

87
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:25,230
they're doing their homework.

88
00:04:25,230 --> 00:04:28,350
But this is a really exciting transition

89
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:31,750
that we're living through.

90
00:04:31,750 --> 00:04:38,370
One of these things that is really driving this, a lot of the scale is driven by GPUs.

91
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:42,990
So in a very real sense, you could say GPUs are the new oil.

92
00:04:42,990 --> 00:04:45,250
This is again an older announcement,

93
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:47,470
but you're seeing hundreds of billions of dollars

94
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:50,950
and more of investment into GPUs.

95
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:55,700
History rhymes in interesting ways with similar things,

96
00:04:55,700 --> 00:04:58,920
but there are entire countries-- entire sovereign wealth funds

97
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:07,120
that are making these things a major part of their piece.

98
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:09,720
And one thing that is really abundantly clear

99
00:05:09,720 --> 00:05:12,660
is that inference is really the piece.

100
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:15,760
So you can think of inference as the engine that turns

101
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,140
electricity into intelligence.

102
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:23,040
So the same way that oil is only useful in a car, if you can have

103
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:27,840
an engine that turns that oil into useful kinetic motion,

104
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:30,040
inference engines, GPU kernels, these

105
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:32,840
are the things that really turn--

106
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:35,260
that really turn these GPUs from sand,

107
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:38,560
like Percy said, into something that we can use today.

108
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:39,860
And I think that's clear.

109
00:05:39,860 --> 00:05:42,360
So if you think about it, these machine learning models,

110
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:44,100
they're really just DAGs of operations.

111
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:47,360
There are some mathematical object that exists in the ether.

112
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:50,880
The inference engines, the GPU kernels, all these pieces, these

113
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:52,880
are the things that you actually have to program

114
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,620
and map them down to ML operations.

115
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:56,560
I think you do some of that in this class.

116
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:58,620
I think you implement some flash attention.

117
00:05:58,620 --> 00:05:59,960
So some training side.

118
00:05:59,960 --> 00:06:01,680
But there's a whole world of complexity

119
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:06,600
when it comes to inference and the inference side of things.

120
00:06:06,600 --> 00:06:10,080
So if you take away nothing from today's talk,

121
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:13,678
I hope there's one thing which is, if you understand inference

122
00:06:13,678 --> 00:06:15,220
and understand the inference engines,

123
00:06:15,220 --> 00:06:17,080
if you understand the GPU kernels

124
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:20,520
that underlie a lot of the core technology,

125
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:23,960
you can enable full stack innovation in machine learning

126
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:24,580
algorithms.

127
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:27,420
So I'm going to start with, in today's talk,

128
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:30,900
a high level overview of the lifetime of a token.

129
00:06:30,900 --> 00:06:34,400
So when you make a request to one of these models, what

130
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:36,520
happens to that request, how it goes

131
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:38,300
through the entire inference service,

132
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:41,200
what are some of the interesting choices you have to make.

133
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:45,040
And then I'll dive deeper into two more research projects

134
00:06:45,040 --> 00:06:50,098
around, if you take certain pieces of this system, what

135
00:06:50,098 --> 00:06:51,890
are questions that you can ask and what are

136
00:06:51,890 --> 00:06:54,970
some things that you can do?

137
00:06:54,970 --> 00:06:57,310
Before I get into all the technical details,

138
00:06:57,310 --> 00:06:58,678
I'll quickly make a plug.

139
00:06:58,678 --> 00:07:00,470
So I'm here representing two organizations.

140
00:07:00,470 --> 00:07:03,930
So one is UCSD, where I have a small lab,

141
00:07:03,930 --> 00:07:07,150
and some of their work is represented in this talk.

142
00:07:07,150 --> 00:07:09,210
Then I'm also representing Together

143
00:07:09,210 --> 00:07:13,410
who did a bunch of the work that's represented in this talk

144
00:07:13,410 --> 00:07:14,950
together as an AI cloud.

145
00:07:14,950 --> 00:07:20,450
So there's GPUs, inference, fine-tuning, and all the rest.

146
00:07:20,450 --> 00:07:21,910
Heavy research background.

147
00:07:21,910 --> 00:07:24,430
So including Percy, I don't think you can see my mouse,

148
00:07:24,430 --> 00:07:26,310
but he's on the screen.

149
00:07:26,310 --> 00:07:29,540
And then we've got a big research presence behind that,

150
00:07:29,540 --> 00:07:31,290
and behind a lot of the things that you're

151
00:07:31,290 --> 00:07:34,130
going to see in this talk.

152
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:34,790
All right.

153
00:07:34,790 --> 00:07:37,930
I'll start by giving a high level overview

154
00:07:37,930 --> 00:07:39,750
of a lifetime of a token.

155
00:07:39,750 --> 00:07:43,550
So when you make a request to an inference system,

156
00:07:43,550 --> 00:07:46,900
what are the different pieces that are going to go through?

157
00:07:46,900 --> 00:07:49,460
And these slides are cribbed from a presentation

158
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:55,340
that my student, Austin, made from his experiences together.

159
00:07:55,340 --> 00:07:57,020
I'll make a note that all of the slides

160
00:07:57,020 --> 00:08:00,580
are completely AI generated from Nano Banana Pro,

161
00:08:00,580 --> 00:08:02,460
but they're pretty good as long as you don't

162
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:04,160
look too closely at the tech.

163
00:08:04,160 --> 00:08:07,160
So I'd say, at a high level everything is correct.

164
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:10,820
If you look very closely, you'll see things that are very wrong.

165
00:08:10,820 --> 00:08:16,760
This is an overview of all the pieces of an inference engine.

166
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:20,840
So I'll give a high level description of how it works.

167
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,157
So you get a request in.

168
00:08:22,157 --> 00:08:23,740
The first thing that's going to happen

169
00:08:23,740 --> 00:08:26,840
is that the request gets scheduled to various different GPUs.

170
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:29,620
You might have disaggregated prefill and decode

171
00:08:29,620 --> 00:08:32,539
on different machines.

172
00:08:32,539 --> 00:08:35,480
You'll run that request against a KV Cache to see,

173
00:08:35,480 --> 00:08:39,020
have I seen this request or versions of this request before?

174
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:44,860
Is there some compute that I can actually save?

175
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:49,380
Then you'll start executing the core machine learning code.

176
00:08:49,380 --> 00:08:50,560
So here are new tokens.

177
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:53,740
Here are new operations that we need to compute.

178
00:08:53,740 --> 00:08:55,960
There are various optimizations that you can make.

179
00:08:55,960 --> 00:08:59,080
So you can split that computation across different machines.

180
00:08:59,080 --> 00:09:00,960
You can parallelize across different nodes.

181
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:04,220
You can parallelize it within a node across different GPUs

182
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:05,840
depending on the size of your model,

183
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:09,060
depending on how you can split up, split that up.

184
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:13,740
And I think one of the exciting things is that, as we are--

185
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,700
as the hardware is developing, we're

186
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:19,340
actually going to start to see more different choices that you

187
00:09:19,340 --> 00:09:21,380
can make there.

188
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,698
But once you make all those choices, once you execute

189
00:09:23,698 --> 00:09:26,240
all the code at the end, you get the tokens that you can say,

190
00:09:26,240 --> 00:09:27,700
hey, Chad, what did Percy mean when

191
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:30,620
he said linear regression because I

192
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:33,100
skipped that day in my class?

193
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:38,140
And you get this whole end to end experience.

194
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:40,140
One of the things that I think is

195
00:09:40,140 --> 00:09:43,350
useful to start thinking about is,

196
00:09:43,350 --> 00:09:46,550
what do these different workloads look like?

197
00:09:46,550 --> 00:09:49,690
And there's quite a bit of different things.

198
00:09:49,690 --> 00:09:52,190
So this is one of the sites where if you look too closely,

199
00:09:52,190 --> 00:09:55,350
I think some of the fives turned into S's.

200
00:09:55,350 --> 00:09:57,750
But one of the things that you want to think about

201
00:09:57,750 --> 00:10:00,390
is, when you're actually serving production traffic,

202
00:10:00,390 --> 00:10:02,390
it doesn't necessarily look like-- certainly,

203
00:10:02,390 --> 00:10:04,630
doesn't look what the type of tokens

204
00:10:04,630 --> 00:10:06,590
that you see during training.

205
00:10:06,590 --> 00:10:11,110
But it also doesn't look like if you just made up a traffic

206
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:12,130
workload in your head.

207
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:15,610
So what is the type thing that we typically see?

208
00:10:15,610 --> 00:10:18,910
So for a particular workload, you'll

209
00:10:18,910 --> 00:10:21,570
see a particular distribution of input and output tokens.

210
00:10:21,570 --> 00:10:24,090
So let's take a coding workload.

211
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:28,630
So let's say you're somebody like Cursor, where you have

212
00:10:28,630 --> 00:10:30,590
your whole code base is available to the agent

213
00:10:30,590 --> 00:10:32,250
and you're asking questions about it,

214
00:10:32,250 --> 00:10:33,750
typically, what that will look like,

215
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:36,410
is that you'll have a long amount of input.

216
00:10:36,410 --> 00:10:39,013
So tens of thousands of input tokens.

217
00:10:39,013 --> 00:10:41,180
And then depending on how you've trained your model,

218
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:43,740
the model might output some amount of thinking tokens,

219
00:10:43,740 --> 00:10:48,460
or it might output some short input in.

220
00:10:48,460 --> 00:10:50,500
And this will change based on workload.

221
00:10:50,500 --> 00:10:53,320
It'll change based on model.

222
00:10:53,320 --> 00:10:57,120
So a coding workload for example will look very different

223
00:10:57,120 --> 00:11:02,020
from a summarization workload, or a narrative summarization.

224
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:06,835
So if your work involves pasting entire books into a chat window

225
00:11:06,835 --> 00:11:09,460
and then talking back and forth to figure out what's happening,

226
00:11:09,460 --> 00:11:12,400
that's going to look very different than a standard chat

227
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:12,900
thing.

228
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:14,340
So if you just go to chat and say,

229
00:11:14,340 --> 00:11:17,840
hey, explain to me first order calculus or whatever,

230
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:21,440
that's going to have very different workload shapes.

231
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:23,220
The way that we use language models today,

232
00:11:23,220 --> 00:11:27,240
there's very turn-based agentic workflows.

233
00:11:27,240 --> 00:11:29,880
So when you're coding, you go back and forth with your coding

234
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:31,340
agent, say, hey, do this.

235
00:11:31,340 --> 00:11:32,740
No, I didn't mean that, do that.

236
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:36,040
Your coding agents themselves can iterate on the language

237
00:11:36,040 --> 00:11:36,940
models quite a bit.

238
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:41,520
So they might invoke some tools and say,

239
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:45,460
grep search for something in my data-- in my code base,

240
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,040
and then take that and go feed it back to the model.

241
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:51,760
I might try to do an internet search and look up this thing

242
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,480
that my user asked me.

243
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:56,720
So typically, you have these multiple turns

244
00:11:56,720 --> 00:11:59,000
in your conversation.

245
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:00,480
Now, another interesting piece is

246
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:02,740
different applications will have different times.

247
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:06,060
So if you're in a fast, interactive chat-based loop,

248
00:12:06,060 --> 00:12:07,920
or if you're talking on your phone

249
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:10,680
to ChatGPT in the voice mode, you

250
00:12:10,680 --> 00:12:13,580
might have relatively quick responses.

251
00:12:13,580 --> 00:12:17,520
If on the other hand, you've put together an agentic workflow

252
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:19,700
where you say, hey, go do this for me,

253
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:22,860
I'm going to leave you alone and just iterate on your own,

254
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:24,340
you'll have a different cadence.

255
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:27,880
If at some point your agent gets stuck and says, hey, help,

256
00:12:27,880 --> 00:12:30,580
I need to ask for advice, and you don't notice it,

257
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:32,920
there might be another gap between turns.

258
00:12:32,920 --> 00:12:35,120
So all of these things start to define

259
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:36,630
what your workload looks like.

260
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:40,070
So how many new tokens do you get in it every time?

261
00:12:40,070 --> 00:12:42,330
How many tokens are you going to generate?

262
00:12:42,330 --> 00:12:43,930
How long is my session?

263
00:12:43,930 --> 00:12:45,610
Am I a very sticky user who keeps

264
00:12:45,610 --> 00:12:47,830
going back and forth with my Claude Agent,

265
00:12:47,830 --> 00:12:51,490
or am I a user who's just going to ask one question

266
00:12:51,490 --> 00:12:54,410
and then leave and come back the next day?

267
00:12:54,410 --> 00:12:57,930
And of course, how long between turns do you have?

268
00:12:57,930 --> 00:13:00,850
I, for example, have a chat that I've

269
00:13:00,850 --> 00:13:04,770
had with a ChatGPT agent about how I should set up my workout

270
00:13:04,770 --> 00:13:07,530
and my workouts week to week.

271
00:13:07,530 --> 00:13:12,210
And I interact with that chat once every other week.

272
00:13:12,210 --> 00:13:14,490
So that is a very different traffic pattern

273
00:13:14,490 --> 00:13:16,527
than some of the other ones.

274
00:13:16,527 --> 00:13:18,610
And then of course, depending on your application,

275
00:13:18,610 --> 00:13:19,985
you might have different targets.

276
00:13:19,985 --> 00:13:23,513
So if you have an interactive application, you might say,

277
00:13:23,513 --> 00:13:25,930
I want to get the first tokens back in less than a second,

278
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:28,650
so that the agent can say, hey, I'm thinking

279
00:13:28,650 --> 00:13:31,250
and I'm going to say blah de blah de blah.

280
00:13:31,250 --> 00:13:34,988
Or I might say, I know I'm going to be generating 500 tokens,

281
00:13:34,988 --> 00:13:36,780
and I want that whole response to come back

282
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:39,447
within a certain amount of time so that my user can read it fast

283
00:13:39,447 --> 00:13:43,120
enough and all these pieces.

284
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:49,980
So as that— when that request comes in,

285
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:52,800
there are a few basic pieces that come in.

286
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:55,820
I'll rest a bit on the prefill and decode piece

287
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:56,680
in a little bit.

288
00:13:56,680 --> 00:13:59,283
So let's say you have some amount of text that comes in,

289
00:13:59,283 --> 00:14:00,700
the first thing you're going to do

290
00:14:00,700 --> 00:14:02,160
is you're going to tokenize it.

291
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:04,140
I think you're all familiar with that.

292
00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:07,340
But then once there, you start getting into a somewhat complex

293
00:14:07,340 --> 00:14:10,220
scheduling regime because you have to ask questions like,

294
00:14:10,220 --> 00:14:12,320
have I seen these tokens before?

295
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:17,100
Can I just look up some of the activations in a cache?

296
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:21,300
And then you get to basically, these two major pieces

297
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:24,820
of the actual machine learning computation.

298
00:14:24,820 --> 00:14:26,623
These are called prefill and decode,

299
00:14:26,623 --> 00:14:28,040
and they're very different beasts.

300
00:14:28,040 --> 00:14:31,220
So prefill means let's say you have 10,000 tokens that

301
00:14:31,220 --> 00:14:35,175
you've never seen before, and you want to go compute what

302
00:14:35,175 --> 00:14:37,050
the activations and what the logit should be.

303
00:14:37,050 --> 00:14:39,550
So 10,000 tokens in, one token out.

304
00:14:39,550 --> 00:14:41,530
That's a very compute bound operation.

305
00:14:41,530 --> 00:14:43,670
So that actually looks pretty close to the things

306
00:14:43,670 --> 00:14:45,730
that you guys have been looking at for training time.

307
00:14:45,730 --> 00:14:47,855
So when you're training, you have some large amount

308
00:14:47,855 --> 00:14:48,570
of tokens.

309
00:14:48,570 --> 00:14:50,112
You write your Flash Attention kernel

310
00:14:50,112 --> 00:14:51,630
when you run your training loop.

311
00:14:51,630 --> 00:14:52,810
Prefill is very similar.

312
00:14:52,810 --> 00:14:54,658
You just don't run your backwards pass.

313
00:14:54,658 --> 00:14:56,450
And then you have this thing called decode.

314
00:14:56,450 --> 00:14:59,190
So decode is when you're then just generating

315
00:14:59,190 --> 00:15:00,090
one token at a time.

316
00:15:00,090 --> 00:15:02,010
So you pass in these 10,000 tokens.

317
00:15:02,010 --> 00:15:03,890
It's like, hey, here's my code base,

318
00:15:03,890 --> 00:15:06,470
tell me what function ABC does?

319
00:15:06,470 --> 00:15:08,710
The model is going to process the whole prompt

320
00:15:08,710 --> 00:15:11,590
and then start generating one token at a time, maybe

321
00:15:11,590 --> 00:15:15,270
three or four tokens if you have speculative decoding working.

322
00:15:15,270 --> 00:15:17,830
And if you think about that operation-- so every time you

323
00:15:17,830 --> 00:15:20,350
generate a new token, you then have to run that back

324
00:15:20,350 --> 00:15:21,250
through the model.

325
00:15:21,250 --> 00:15:23,625
If you do the math on that, there's actually not too many

326
00:15:23,625 --> 00:15:25,430
flops that you need to compute.

327
00:15:25,430 --> 00:15:29,310
So it's going to be a relatively light computation,

328
00:15:29,310 --> 00:15:31,450
but it's going to be very memory bandwidth bound.

329
00:15:31,450 --> 00:15:32,867
So what that means is you're going

330
00:15:32,867 --> 00:15:34,950
to be running-- you have to load up the model

331
00:15:34,950 --> 00:15:37,910
every time just to generate a single token.

332
00:15:37,910 --> 00:15:39,342
At the end, you get--

333
00:15:39,342 --> 00:15:41,050
at the end of that model pass, of course,

334
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:43,990
you then have a single number that

335
00:15:43,990 --> 00:15:47,330
represents a token that then gets turned into a string.

336
00:15:47,330 --> 00:15:49,930
You probably do a little bit of processing on that.

337
00:15:49,930 --> 00:15:51,610
So you look for stop tokens.

338
00:15:51,610 --> 00:15:52,150
You check.

339
00:15:52,150 --> 00:15:55,910
You might run a safety check to say, oh,

340
00:15:55,910 --> 00:15:58,910
is my user trying to hack into the system

341
00:15:58,910 --> 00:16:00,650
in a bad way, et cetera?

342
00:16:00,650 --> 00:16:04,670
And then at the end, you get the tokens.

343
00:16:04,670 --> 00:16:07,830
And the inference engine is really running in this loop,

344
00:16:07,830 --> 00:16:10,270
waiting for these requests to come in.

345
00:16:10,270 --> 00:16:13,350
So it's waiting-- so it's running this scheduling

346
00:16:13,350 --> 00:16:17,650
execution token sampling loop and repeating.

347
00:16:20,950 --> 00:16:23,670
So I'll get into a little bit of-- so now we

348
00:16:23,670 --> 00:16:26,450
can take one step deeper and start to look at,

349
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:27,950
what does it look like when you have

350
00:16:27,950 --> 00:16:31,560
a system that is processing many different requests at a time?

351
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:34,360
So we have this phenomenon called-- this technique called

352
00:16:34,360 --> 00:16:35,640
continuous batching.

353
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:39,080
So the way to read this figure is that it

354
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:40,980
is time is flowing downward.

355
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:44,880
So as you go down, there are new requests that are coming in.

356
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:46,600
So let's say, if we go to step one,

357
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:47,820
you have some long requests.

358
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:54,920
You have a user that is asking to analyze some long document,

359
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:58,920
and the engine is going and generating a bunch of tokens out.

360
00:16:58,920 --> 00:17:02,400
You might have another request comes in that starts

361
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:04,339
to take up some resources.

362
00:17:04,339 --> 00:17:06,579
So these resources are first compute resources.

363
00:17:06,579 --> 00:17:10,980
You have to run things over multiple requests at once.

364
00:17:10,980 --> 00:17:14,960
They can be memory resources if you're filling up a KV Cache.

365
00:17:14,960 --> 00:17:18,040
And so you'll see these multiple requests

366
00:17:18,040 --> 00:17:20,800
happening at the same time.

367
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:23,240
After a step, that short request might finish

368
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:26,329
and you get a new request that comes in,

369
00:17:26,329 --> 00:17:29,330
and maybe it's running for a couple steps.

370
00:17:29,330 --> 00:17:31,730
If you have another request that comes in,

371
00:17:31,730 --> 00:17:33,505
maybe it's another very long request.

372
00:17:33,505 --> 00:17:35,630
So that's going to be that orange one in step four.

373
00:17:35,630 --> 00:17:37,430
But maybe you don't have enough GPU memory.

374
00:17:37,430 --> 00:17:40,510
So you need to store all your KV Cache on your GPU.

375
00:17:40,510 --> 00:17:41,630
Maybe you've run out.

376
00:17:41,630 --> 00:17:45,450
You might start queuing for some reason there.

377
00:17:45,450 --> 00:17:47,190
Then once that long request is done,

378
00:17:47,190 --> 00:17:49,770
you can start the new request, and then, et cetera,

379
00:17:49,770 --> 00:17:51,010
you can watch it run.

380
00:17:51,010 --> 00:17:53,010
So already, you're seeing some of the complexity

381
00:17:53,010 --> 00:17:57,490
when you have many different requests living in a system

382
00:17:57,490 --> 00:18:02,250
and how it goes on.

383
00:18:02,250 --> 00:18:05,170
One of the pieces that's quite important

384
00:18:05,170 --> 00:18:08,370
is this thing called a KV Cache.

385
00:18:08,370 --> 00:18:12,210
So the way to think about this is that you probably

386
00:18:12,210 --> 00:18:15,130
have a lot of users who are saying, hi, ChatGPT

387
00:18:15,130 --> 00:18:16,230
or hi, Claude.

388
00:18:16,230 --> 00:18:18,570
And then theoretically, you don't

389
00:18:18,570 --> 00:18:22,170
need to compute new activations and run that again

390
00:18:22,170 --> 00:18:24,530
for every single user.

391
00:18:24,530 --> 00:18:28,490
Or if you have a long book that the user has passed in,

392
00:18:28,490 --> 00:18:31,150
you compute, you run the prefill over that once,

393
00:18:31,150 --> 00:18:34,230
and then the next time you get some addendum to that request.

394
00:18:34,230 --> 00:18:35,750
The next turn in the conversation,

395
00:18:35,750 --> 00:18:38,530
you don't need to compute the whole thing again.

396
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:42,370
So we have these mechanisms called KV Caches

397
00:18:42,370 --> 00:18:44,710
that use prefix sharing, that say, hey,

398
00:18:44,710 --> 00:18:46,890
I have this new set of tokens that comes in,

399
00:18:46,890 --> 00:18:50,570
use a very traditional data structure like a basic tree,

400
00:18:50,570 --> 00:18:55,010
look at which tokens have I seen before, which tokens are new,

401
00:18:55,010 --> 00:18:57,650
and then basically, do a lookup of what those activations are

402
00:18:57,650 --> 00:19:00,930
going to look like.

403
00:19:00,930 --> 00:19:04,310
Once you actually have the computation on the GPUs,

404
00:19:04,310 --> 00:19:06,330
there's various ways to split it.

405
00:19:06,330 --> 00:19:08,750
So I'm not sure if you talked about this too much,

406
00:19:08,750 --> 00:19:11,530
but let's say you have a trillion parameter model

407
00:19:11,530 --> 00:19:16,433
and you're running on 280 gigabyte GPUs,

408
00:19:16,433 --> 00:19:17,850
you're not going to be able to fit

409
00:19:17,850 --> 00:19:21,317
the whole model onto each GPU.

410
00:19:21,317 --> 00:19:23,400
So there are various ways that you might split it.

411
00:19:23,400 --> 00:19:28,280
So you might take every tensor and split four ways across four GPUs.

412
00:19:28,280 --> 00:19:29,980
This is called tensor parallelism.

413
00:19:29,980 --> 00:19:33,500
Or today, we have a lot of the state of the art models

414
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:35,120
or a mixture of experts models.

415
00:19:35,120 --> 00:19:37,580
So you have these individual experts

416
00:19:37,580 --> 00:19:40,520
that get selectively activated depending on different tokens.

417
00:19:40,520 --> 00:19:42,940
You can split those across different GPUs.

418
00:19:42,940 --> 00:19:44,980
The choices that you make at this point

419
00:19:44,980 --> 00:19:46,940
will determine what are the bottlenecks.

420
00:19:46,940 --> 00:19:52,140
How many GPUs do you need to run your model?

421
00:19:52,140 --> 00:19:55,060
How many sessions can you serve at the same time?

422
00:19:55,060 --> 00:19:58,060
Et cetera.

423
00:19:58,060 --> 00:20:00,060
One of the big things that happens

424
00:20:00,060 --> 00:20:05,000
is that we tend to split prefill and decode onto different GPUs,

425
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,500
different sets of machines, and that's

426
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:10,500
because they have very different compute bottlenecks

427
00:20:10,500 --> 00:20:11,840
and compute characteristics.

428
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:14,980
So prefill looks a lot like what you guys do during training.

429
00:20:14,980 --> 00:20:16,160
You just run it.

430
00:20:16,160 --> 00:20:17,980
It's very flop heavy.

431
00:20:17,980 --> 00:20:20,950
You can really use the most out of your GPUs.

432
00:20:20,950 --> 00:20:23,858
Decode on the other hand is very memory bandwidth heavy.

433
00:20:23,858 --> 00:20:25,650
Because you're not doing that much compute,

434
00:20:25,650 --> 00:20:30,230
but you still have to load up all the model

435
00:20:30,230 --> 00:20:31,360
weights at the same time.

436
00:20:31,360 --> 00:20:33,610
These things will also take different amounts of time.

437
00:20:33,610 --> 00:20:36,990
So prefill will typically take a lot longer than a single decode

438
00:20:36,990 --> 00:20:37,498
step.

439
00:20:37,498 --> 00:20:39,790
But you're going to be running a lot more decode steps,

440
00:20:39,790 --> 00:20:41,930
because you will run prefill once for a prompt,

441
00:20:41,930 --> 00:20:46,190
you'll run decode once for every token that you generate.

442
00:20:46,190 --> 00:20:49,932
So a very basic optimization that pretty much we've

443
00:20:49,932 --> 00:20:51,390
all started adopting is that you'll

444
00:20:51,390 --> 00:20:53,690
run prefill on one set of workers,

445
00:20:53,690 --> 00:20:55,790
decode on another set of workers,

446
00:20:55,790 --> 00:20:57,470
so that you can specialize those two

447
00:20:57,470 --> 00:21:00,990
computations to different pieces of the stack.

448
00:21:00,990 --> 00:21:03,110
And turns out, there's a lot of innovation

449
00:21:03,110 --> 00:21:05,722
that you can have when you have these splits.

450
00:21:05,722 --> 00:21:07,430
Some things that you might have heard of,

451
00:21:07,430 --> 00:21:09,610
that when NVIDIA bought Grok.

452
00:21:09,610 --> 00:21:15,530
So NVIDIA, the King of GPUs, buys this new inference chip.

453
00:21:15,530 --> 00:21:18,630
One of the reasons is that the decode workload

454
00:21:18,630 --> 00:21:20,470
is so different from prefill workload,

455
00:21:20,470 --> 00:21:22,870
that if you're looking at code, you can

456
00:21:22,870 --> 00:21:24,910
be using very different chips.

457
00:21:24,910 --> 00:21:27,610
So for example, in the next generation of hardware,

458
00:21:27,610 --> 00:21:31,510
NVIDIA is planning on using its GPUs for the pre-fill side,

459
00:21:31,510 --> 00:21:34,730
using these LPU Grok chips for the decode.

460
00:21:34,730 --> 00:21:37,850
You see similar things with Cerebras.

461
00:21:37,850 --> 00:21:40,770
So OpenAI has this compute partnership with Cerebras.

462
00:21:40,770 --> 00:21:43,350
It's another chip that's much better at decode.

463
00:21:43,350 --> 00:21:45,030
There are other companies like SambaNova

464
00:21:45,030 --> 00:21:47,430
and so on, that are also making bets

465
00:21:47,430 --> 00:21:49,370
on various parts of this space.

466
00:21:52,930 --> 00:21:54,690
Here's a fun one, an interesting one.

467
00:21:54,690 --> 00:21:58,150
So when you actually start deploying

468
00:21:58,150 --> 00:21:59,850
these inference engines at scale,

469
00:21:59,850 --> 00:22:02,710
so when you're starting to serve trillions of tokens or more

470
00:22:02,710 --> 00:22:05,690
a day, you start to get some pretty nasty bugs.

471
00:22:05,690 --> 00:22:07,210
So this is a set of bugs.

472
00:22:07,210 --> 00:22:12,090
I think most of these happened in

473
00:22:12,090 --> 00:22:16,600
late last year with some of the open source inference engines.

474
00:22:16,600 --> 00:22:22,000
And basically, one of the characteristics

475
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,840
of these large scale systems is that something

476
00:22:24,840 --> 00:22:26,400
that will work well at a small scale

477
00:22:26,400 --> 00:22:28,820
will inevitably start breaking at a large scale.

478
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:33,280
So we're talking events that happen with 0.001% of the time

479
00:22:33,280 --> 00:22:34,560
or less.

480
00:22:34,560 --> 00:22:37,740
Some of these can be so NaN.

481
00:22:37,740 --> 00:22:39,920
So sometimes, you'll have a kernel

482
00:22:39,920 --> 00:22:41,620
that is very slightly wrong.

483
00:22:41,620 --> 00:22:46,040
But the conditions for triggering it are very rare.

484
00:22:46,040 --> 00:22:49,320
And you will start having some of your logits

485
00:22:49,320 --> 00:22:53,740
turn into NaNs halfway through the computation.

486
00:22:53,740 --> 00:22:55,560
When that happens, we figure it out

487
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:57,940
that the model starts outputting the same token.

488
00:22:57,940 --> 00:23:02,600
So I think some model, the output just started saying, hi, hi, hi, hi, after a while,

489
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:05,480
or start outputting exclamation points

490
00:23:05,480 --> 00:23:09,280
and you get caught into these loops.

491
00:23:09,280 --> 00:23:11,880
Another model, at some point, someone

492
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:14,620
made a change to how you're handling tool calls.

493
00:23:14,620 --> 00:23:17,990
So tool calls are when the model says,

494
00:23:17,990 --> 00:23:21,590
hey, I want to make an internet search or something like that.

495
00:23:21,590 --> 00:23:24,110
So the model will actually say, go do an internet search.

496
00:23:24,110 --> 00:23:28,290
And then your end harness has to then go use old fashioned code

497
00:23:28,290 --> 00:23:30,290
to do the internet search.

498
00:23:30,290 --> 00:23:35,610
At some point, those tool calls stop being processed correctly

499
00:23:35,610 --> 00:23:36,710
in one of the engines.

500
00:23:36,710 --> 00:23:39,730
And the symptom for that the completion lengths shot up

501
00:23:39,730 --> 00:23:43,390
because the model would say, hey, make an internet search,

502
00:23:43,390 --> 00:23:46,768
and then it would usually--

503
00:23:46,768 --> 00:23:48,310
when the model is behaving correctly,

504
00:23:48,310 --> 00:23:50,050
it says, hey, make an internet search.

505
00:23:50,050 --> 00:23:50,550
I'm done.

506
00:23:50,550 --> 00:23:52,010
Go return to the user.

507
00:23:52,010 --> 00:23:53,190
Go do that, whatever.

508
00:23:53,190 --> 00:23:55,590
But in this case, it wasn't returning correctly.

509
00:23:55,590 --> 00:23:57,390
So it'd say, hey, make an internet search.

510
00:23:57,390 --> 00:23:58,750
Hey, make an internet search.

511
00:23:58,750 --> 00:24:01,167
Hey, I don't know why there's no internet search going on.

512
00:24:01,167 --> 00:24:03,010
It would just get into this very long doom

513
00:24:03,010 --> 00:24:07,250
loop for tens of thousands of tokens.

514
00:24:07,250 --> 00:24:10,490
There's another one that--

515
00:24:10,490 --> 00:24:13,900
this one was interesting because it actually

516
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:17,940
took out a number of insurance providers at the same time.

517
00:24:17,940 --> 00:24:21,380
And it actually got blamed on a quantization issue.

518
00:24:21,380 --> 00:24:23,980
But actually, what was happening was a much more subtle bug

519
00:24:23,980 --> 00:24:28,100
where the model would just suddenly, randomly

520
00:24:28,100 --> 00:24:32,320
start to output Chinese characters when it had no reason to.

521
00:24:32,320 --> 00:24:37,820
So for some models, that sometimes there's

522
00:24:37,820 --> 00:24:39,528
some speculation, oh, they must have

523
00:24:39,528 --> 00:24:41,820
fine-tuned on a Chinese model because my model suddenly

524
00:24:41,820 --> 00:24:42,440
starts.

525
00:24:42,440 --> 00:24:44,260
I ask it a question in English and starts

526
00:24:44,260 --> 00:24:46,900
speaking back to me in Chinese.

527
00:24:46,900 --> 00:24:50,140
Actually, what was happening is that there was just an off-by-one

528
00:24:50,140 --> 00:24:53,000
error in one of the kernels, and it would be a very subtle bug.

529
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:58,700
So sometimes, you would read in some extra uninitialized memory

530
00:24:58,700 --> 00:25:02,545
space from your GPU, run it through retention, and then

531
00:25:02,545 --> 00:25:03,920
at the end of that whole process,

532
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:05,600
you get a random Chinese character.

533
00:25:05,600 --> 00:25:07,980
Then the model will go, why did I start suddenly

534
00:25:07,980 --> 00:25:09,660
thinking in Chinese?

535
00:25:09,660 --> 00:25:12,700
I must be-- the user must be asking me a question in Chinese,

536
00:25:12,700 --> 00:25:15,340
and then it will just go veer off into Chinese.

537
00:25:15,340 --> 00:25:17,820
So sometimes when this happens, it's

538
00:25:17,820 --> 00:25:21,500
because the model has legitimately been trained to say--

539
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:23,160
to think in Chinese.

540
00:25:23,160 --> 00:25:27,400
Sometimes, it can just be an off-by-one bug in somebody's code.

541
00:25:31,780 --> 00:25:33,780
Some interesting things that are starting

542
00:25:33,780 --> 00:25:38,180
to happen in a lot of the more advanced inference stacks.

543
00:25:38,180 --> 00:25:43,060
So a major piece of running large production systems

544
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:46,960
is that you want to have as large of a KV Cache as possible.

545
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:50,660
So it is best if you can cache requests

546
00:25:50,660 --> 00:25:53,140
from many different users or from the same user

547
00:25:53,140 --> 00:25:56,700
across many different sessions, and be able to run

548
00:25:56,700 --> 00:25:59,100
as many sessions as possible.

549
00:25:59,100 --> 00:26:03,020
You first started this by storing your KV Cache on GPU,

550
00:26:03,020 --> 00:26:05,000
and then you quickly run out of GPU memory.

551
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:08,460
So the next, you can start storing into CPU DRAM.

552
00:26:08,460 --> 00:26:12,990
So if you're paying attention to Jensen's keynotes,

553
00:26:12,990 --> 00:26:15,590
he's recently started getting very obsessed

554
00:26:15,590 --> 00:26:18,030
with CPU performance.

555
00:26:18,030 --> 00:26:22,750
One of the reasons is because a past generation of CPUs

556
00:26:22,750 --> 00:26:25,030
was actually really slow.

557
00:26:25,030 --> 00:26:28,390
And as a result, started bottlenecking a bunch

558
00:26:28,390 --> 00:26:31,230
of very important workloads.

559
00:26:31,230 --> 00:26:33,470
And as a result, you start to pay attention

560
00:26:33,470 --> 00:26:37,630
because if you're $500,000 machine is being bottlenecked

561
00:26:37,630 --> 00:26:42,470
by the $1,000 CPU that you purchased to put on top of it,

562
00:26:42,470 --> 00:26:44,013
that's not a great place.

563
00:26:44,013 --> 00:26:45,430
One of the reasons that can happen

564
00:26:45,430 --> 00:26:49,030
is that you might be storing your KV cache on CPU memory.

565
00:26:49,030 --> 00:26:50,630
And so you really care about the speed

566
00:26:50,630 --> 00:26:53,270
of being able to read that KV cache back.

567
00:26:53,270 --> 00:26:57,470
Next, you might put more KV cache onto the disk itself.

568
00:26:57,470 --> 00:27:01,590
And then you start caring about SSDs and SSD space.

569
00:27:01,590 --> 00:27:04,790
Again, you've probably heard these rumblings about OpenAI

570
00:27:04,790 --> 00:27:08,280
buying up all the SSD, all the DRAM in the world.

571
00:27:08,280 --> 00:27:10,600
Part of the reason is for stuff like this,

572
00:27:10,600 --> 00:27:13,200
where you want to store as much stuff in your KV cache

573
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:15,117
as you can.

574
00:27:15,117 --> 00:27:17,700
And then of course, when you're actually building your engine,

575
00:27:17,700 --> 00:27:19,560
there's this complicated dance of,

576
00:27:19,560 --> 00:27:22,820
I haven't seen these tokens in a while.

577
00:27:22,820 --> 00:27:25,400
Maybe I'll evict it, send it on to the CPU,

578
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:29,280
or send it into disk, or send it into some other global store.

579
00:27:29,280 --> 00:27:31,620
When I get a new request in, I have to go look.

580
00:27:31,620 --> 00:27:33,560
I have to go wait for those to come in,

581
00:27:33,560 --> 00:27:39,680
fetch them, load them up into my thing, and then we're happy.

582
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:42,340
It's kind of CPU offloading.

583
00:27:42,340 --> 00:27:44,940
Is it like a special kind of workload that gets offloaded?

584
00:27:44,940 --> 00:27:47,920
For something fast, you don't want to do this.

585
00:27:47,920 --> 00:27:50,013
Time to hit an SSD is very long.

586
00:27:50,013 --> 00:27:51,180
Can you repeat the question?

587
00:27:51,180 --> 00:27:51,680
Yes.

588
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:54,900
Yeah, so the question is, so for this offloading,

589
00:27:54,900 --> 00:27:58,480
is it a particular type of workload that you offload?

590
00:27:58,480 --> 00:28:00,785
So here, we actually get to some pretty--

591
00:28:00,785 --> 00:28:03,160
I'm sure none of you guys have taken an operating systems

592
00:28:03,160 --> 00:28:03,660
class.

593
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:06,320
But you should take your operating systems classes--

594
00:28:06,320 --> 00:28:08,980
you actually get to some pretty classic scheduling things.

595
00:28:08,980 --> 00:28:13,320
So you could have this diagram except for the GPU

596
00:28:13,320 --> 00:28:14,340
things on the right.

597
00:28:14,340 --> 00:28:18,360
Looks exactly like an operating systems diagram that you might

598
00:28:18,360 --> 00:28:21,040
have seen in the '70s or the '80s.

599
00:28:21,040 --> 00:28:23,600
Because we used to have this problem, where if you opened up

600
00:28:23,600 --> 00:28:26,080
too many applications on your computer,

601
00:28:26,080 --> 00:28:28,240
you would run out of CPU memory, and then

602
00:28:28,240 --> 00:28:32,640
you'd have to put those applications onto the disk.

603
00:28:32,640 --> 00:28:35,080
And it is exactly the same workload.

604
00:28:35,080 --> 00:28:40,520
So ideally, here, actually on the left side,

605
00:28:40,520 --> 00:28:42,880
the Nano Banana hallucinated evictions

606
00:28:42,880 --> 00:28:44,600
for least recently used, which is actually

607
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:46,880
a pretty decent heuristic.

608
00:28:46,880 --> 00:28:50,080
There's probably some OS paper somewhere that says that LRU is

609
00:28:50,080 --> 00:28:51,820
within 2x of what you can do.

610
00:28:51,820 --> 00:28:52,525
That's optimal.

611
00:28:52,525 --> 00:28:54,400
Of course, the best thing that you want to do

612
00:28:54,400 --> 00:28:57,400
is, if you could predict the future, you would know,

613
00:28:57,400 --> 00:29:01,940
oh, I'm about to have a request come in of a particular kind.

614
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:05,370
Let me go prefetch that memory in.

615
00:29:05,370 --> 00:29:07,590
It might actually be possible to predict the future.

616
00:29:07,590 --> 00:29:09,730
So for example, when you go into your chat app

617
00:29:09,730 --> 00:29:13,145
and you bring up some old conversation from a month ago,

618
00:29:13,145 --> 00:29:14,770
that's a very strong signal that you're

619
00:29:14,770 --> 00:29:16,910
going to go start asking a question about it.

620
00:29:16,910 --> 00:29:21,490
You might then want to go load that up onto a GPU.

621
00:29:21,490 --> 00:29:24,750
But in a perfect world, you predict the future.

622
00:29:24,750 --> 00:29:28,010
If you can't predict the future, you use various heuristics.

623
00:29:28,010 --> 00:29:34,050
And really, it's a question of how much traffic

624
00:29:34,050 --> 00:29:37,250
you want to put onto your GPU footprint.

625
00:29:37,250 --> 00:29:39,130
I've never talked to anybody who wants to put

626
00:29:39,130 --> 00:29:41,450
less traffic onto their GPUs.

627
00:29:41,450 --> 00:29:45,490
So I think modulo, or subject to, those SLAs

628
00:29:45,490 --> 00:29:48,930
that we splashed up at the beginning,

629
00:29:48,930 --> 00:29:50,990
you want to serve as much traffic as you can.

630
00:29:54,010 --> 00:29:57,110
Some fun things that are starting to happen.

631
00:29:57,110 --> 00:30:00,730
So in the last generation of Blackwell GPUs,

632
00:30:00,730 --> 00:30:04,860
NVIDIA started putting together these new GPUs called

633
00:30:04,860 --> 00:30:06,860
NVL72 Grace Blackwell chips.

634
00:30:06,860 --> 00:30:09,340
So these are 72 GPUs that are connected

635
00:30:09,340 --> 00:30:11,860
with really fast interconnects.

636
00:30:11,860 --> 00:30:13,500
And so you start to do things like,

637
00:30:13,500 --> 00:30:17,300
how can I split my trillion parameter model across all 72

638
00:30:17,300 --> 00:30:18,180
GPUs?

639
00:30:18,180 --> 00:30:19,940
Does this make any sense?

640
00:30:19,940 --> 00:30:22,500
What does it buy me?

641
00:30:22,500 --> 00:30:23,900
What happens?

642
00:30:23,900 --> 00:30:26,280
How do I start to think about fault tolerance?

643
00:30:26,280 --> 00:30:30,820
So these things can fail a lot for various different reasons.

644
00:30:30,820 --> 00:30:34,320
One reason is that the connectors are flimsy.

645
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:36,400
They're made of plastic, not made of metal.

646
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:38,240
So if you like jam the thing in too much,

647
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:41,100
then your cables are going to bend the things a little bit,

648
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:45,860
and then you get really flaky NV links, which

649
00:30:45,860 --> 00:30:47,560
is a whole thing in itself.

650
00:30:47,560 --> 00:30:50,480
But what happens when--so this is the AI generation.

651
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:53,780
So let's put a fan into the chips, which doesn't quite

652
00:30:53,780 --> 00:30:55,088
make sense.

653
00:30:55,088 --> 00:30:57,380
One of the things that you want to start thinking about

654
00:30:57,380 --> 00:31:00,480
is, if I've taken a model split it across 64 GPUs,

655
00:31:00,480 --> 00:31:03,020
and I'm serving production traffic against millions

656
00:31:03,020 --> 00:31:05,160
of users, trillions of tokens.

657
00:31:05,160 --> 00:31:07,883
What do I do when a single GPU goes down?

658
00:31:07,883 --> 00:31:09,800
Is there some way to make that fault tolerant?

659
00:31:09,800 --> 00:31:14,020
And there's a lot of fun things to think about there.

660
00:31:14,020 --> 00:31:15,660
And then, of course, you're starting

661
00:31:15,660 --> 00:31:18,860
to see these models have a million contexts or more.

662
00:31:18,860 --> 00:31:20,920
How do you actually process it?

663
00:31:20,920 --> 00:31:24,460
Do you split that context across many different GPUs?

664
00:31:24,460 --> 00:31:26,200
How do you do these different things?

665
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:33,780
So I want to dive--just very briefly highlight one set--

666
00:31:33,780 --> 00:31:36,100
one example of an optimization that you

667
00:31:36,100 --> 00:31:39,660
can start to make when you start looking

668
00:31:39,660 --> 00:31:42,940
at this whole process from a systems level.

669
00:31:42,940 --> 00:31:47,140
So this is a piece of work that we put together

670
00:31:47,140 --> 00:31:49,780
a few months ago called Cache-aware Prefill-decode

671
00:31:49,780 --> 00:31:51,220
Disaggregation.

672
00:31:51,220 --> 00:31:52,720
It's a very simple optimization.

673
00:31:52,720 --> 00:31:55,620
It's two lines of code in the routing layer,

674
00:31:55,620 --> 00:31:57,720
but can actually make a pretty big difference.

675
00:31:57,720 --> 00:32:01,350
So the basic idea is, you have all these requests that

676
00:32:01,350 --> 00:32:02,230
are coming in.

677
00:32:02,230 --> 00:32:06,838
Most of them are going to be turn by turn requests

678
00:32:06,838 --> 00:32:08,630
in a conversation, where somebody's already

679
00:32:08,630 --> 00:32:10,710
started a conversation.

680
00:32:10,710 --> 00:32:13,990
But let's say your average conversation lasts 10 turns

681
00:32:13,990 --> 00:32:16,230
and then the user goes away, that suggests

682
00:32:16,230 --> 00:32:19,590
that 10% of your requests are going to be very fresh, very

683
00:32:19,590 --> 00:32:20,595
new requests.

684
00:32:20,595 --> 00:32:22,470
So when you have a new request that comes in,

685
00:32:22,470 --> 00:32:24,850
that's going to be thousands of tokens.

686
00:32:24,850 --> 00:32:26,430
It's going to look very different.

687
00:32:26,430 --> 00:32:29,090
It's going to be a lot more expensive to compute.

688
00:32:29,090 --> 00:32:31,670
You don't necessarily want to put that prefill

689
00:32:31,670 --> 00:32:35,830
against a short conversation where you're halfway

690
00:32:35,830 --> 00:32:36,810
through conversation.

691
00:32:36,810 --> 00:32:39,010
So someone paste in a book and says,

692
00:32:39,010 --> 00:32:40,450
hey, talk to me about this book.

693
00:32:40,450 --> 00:32:42,350
You don't want that running at the same time

694
00:32:42,350 --> 00:32:44,490
as someone who's midway through a conversation,

695
00:32:44,490 --> 00:32:50,190
like, chat explain to me why 1 plus 1 equals 2.

696
00:32:50,190 --> 00:32:51,710
And chat is like, 1 plus 1 equals 2

697
00:32:51,710 --> 00:32:52,990
because numbers and whatnot.

698
00:32:52,990 --> 00:32:54,490
And you're like, oh, I don't get it.

699
00:32:54,490 --> 00:32:58,040
You don't want that very short question and answering

700
00:32:58,040 --> 00:33:00,720
to happen in the same time, on the same GPUs,

701
00:33:00,720 --> 00:33:06,240
as the very long request.

702
00:33:06,240 --> 00:33:09,400
So you can put together this really simple router that

703
00:33:09,400 --> 00:33:11,440
says, OK, if we have a new request that

704
00:33:11,440 --> 00:33:13,980
comes in that's very low cache hit rate,

705
00:33:13,980 --> 00:33:16,840
send it to one set of GPUs so that those can all

706
00:33:16,840 --> 00:33:20,360
process things together and then send all my other warm requests

707
00:33:20,360 --> 00:33:22,488
to another set of prefill nodes.

708
00:33:22,488 --> 00:33:24,280
It turns out if you do this, you can get up

709
00:33:24,280 --> 00:33:30,700
to 40% faster serving with these very, very simple optimization.

710
00:33:30,700 --> 00:33:33,080
So that's one set of things I'd say,

711
00:33:33,080 --> 00:33:34,880
the way that I would characterize

712
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:37,880
where we are in terms of the research

713
00:33:37,880 --> 00:33:39,860
and these techniques is that we're very early.

714
00:33:39,860 --> 00:33:44,680
So this is the type of thing that in 10, 20 years,

715
00:33:44,680 --> 00:33:47,360
they're going to look back on and be like, oh, why are

716
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:48,720
these guys talking about this?

717
00:33:48,720 --> 00:33:51,920
Isn't this already obvious to folks?

718
00:33:51,920 --> 00:33:54,750
But really, it's because we're starting to see these things,

719
00:33:54,750 --> 00:33:56,990
running them at production in new ways.

720
00:33:56,990 --> 00:34:03,530
Seeing these new bits of traffic in new ways.

721
00:34:03,530 --> 00:34:04,030
All right.

722
00:34:04,030 --> 00:34:09,330
So that was an overview of the inference lifetime of a kernel.

723
00:34:09,330 --> 00:34:11,690
Now I'm going to talk about two interesting research

724
00:34:11,690 --> 00:34:15,409
projects that are very much inspired by some of the things

725
00:34:15,409 --> 00:34:17,810
that we see when serving things.

726
00:34:17,810 --> 00:34:22,090
So this first one we're going to be talking about language model

727
00:34:22,090 --> 00:34:24,290
decode, and how you can make it go

728
00:34:24,290 --> 00:34:27,090
a lot faster with these things called mega kernels.

729
00:34:27,090 --> 00:34:32,090
This is a collaboration between Stanford and Together.

730
00:34:32,090 --> 00:34:34,429
So the fundamental challenge when you're running decode.

731
00:34:34,429 --> 00:34:36,969
So decode is the process by which you've processed your prompt

732
00:34:36,969 --> 00:34:39,290
and now you're generating one token at a time.

733
00:34:39,290 --> 00:34:42,850
The fundamental challenge is that you

734
00:34:42,850 --> 00:34:46,969
have to run the whole model to generate a single token.

735
00:34:46,969 --> 00:34:50,770
So that means that instead of using all this big parallelism

736
00:34:50,770 --> 00:34:52,810
that you get with a GPU that you can

737
00:34:52,810 --> 00:34:54,850
do during pre-fill training, you've

738
00:34:54,850 --> 00:34:58,770
now turned this massively parallel system into basically

739
00:34:58,770 --> 00:35:03,130
a glorified memory loader.

740
00:35:03,130 --> 00:35:06,090
And one of the things that makes this extra challenging

741
00:35:06,090 --> 00:35:08,370
is that the way that we typically

742
00:35:08,370 --> 00:35:10,970
write down kernels and run a model

743
00:35:10,970 --> 00:35:14,210
is that we will write down kernels and program

744
00:35:14,210 --> 00:35:16,370
things one operation at a time.

745
00:35:16,370 --> 00:35:18,950
So you guys can probably understand why this is so.

746
00:35:18,950 --> 00:35:21,390
Kernels tend to be pretty challenging to write.

747
00:35:21,390 --> 00:35:25,330
I'm sure you guys all had a lot of fun writing Flash Attention.

748
00:35:25,330 --> 00:35:27,210
But that means that typically, what we do is

749
00:35:27,210 --> 00:35:30,790
we look at all the different operations in a language model,

750
00:35:30,790 --> 00:35:32,370
and we will run—

751
00:35:32,370 --> 00:35:36,730
we will write a single kernel for that operation at a time.

752
00:35:36,730 --> 00:35:39,530
So this makes things a lot easier to program

753
00:35:39,530 --> 00:35:41,770
because you just have to write your norm

754
00:35:41,770 --> 00:35:45,770
kernel, your matmul kernel, your attention kernel.

755
00:35:45,770 --> 00:35:52,020
But you end up seeing, it ends up introducing a lot of downtime

756
00:35:52,020 --> 00:35:53,940
into your system.

757
00:35:53,940 --> 00:35:56,860
So this is an example of what it might

758
00:35:56,860 --> 00:35:59,720
look like when you're running inference across your kernel.

759
00:35:59,720 --> 00:36:03,500
So this is obviously a cartoon, but the example

760
00:36:03,500 --> 00:36:09,020
is drawn from a particular attention inference kernel.

761
00:36:09,020 --> 00:36:12,120
The way to read this is that on the x-axis, you have time.

762
00:36:12,120 --> 00:36:14,240
So time is flowing from left to right.

763
00:36:14,240 --> 00:36:18,860
On the y-axis, you have all the different little streaming

764
00:36:18,860 --> 00:36:20,800
multiprocessors on your GPU.

765
00:36:20,800 --> 00:36:23,940
So on an H100, there are 132 of these.

766
00:36:23,940 --> 00:36:27,180
On the B200, there's 148, et cetera.

767
00:36:27,180 --> 00:36:29,420
The bars indicate useful work.

768
00:36:29,420 --> 00:36:35,020
So when there's a bar here, one of the processors on the GPU

769
00:36:35,020 --> 00:36:38,920
is actually doing work, and the empty space is just waiting.

770
00:36:38,920 --> 00:36:40,820
So you're just waiting for other operations

771
00:36:40,820 --> 00:36:44,980
to finish so that you can have something else go on.

772
00:36:44,980 --> 00:36:48,670
And so basically, you get into this position

773
00:36:48,670 --> 00:36:51,808
where no matter how well you try to write the kernel,

774
00:36:51,808 --> 00:36:53,850
you're always going to have downtime in your GPU.

775
00:36:53,850 --> 00:36:55,933
So you're going to have things like kernel launch.

776
00:36:55,933 --> 00:36:57,690
So a kernel launch or kernel teardown,

777
00:36:57,690 --> 00:37:00,800
that's these big gaps in the red and the yellow.

778
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:03,050
You're going to have these things called tail effects.

779
00:37:03,050 --> 00:37:06,550
So this is just the same way that if you

780
00:37:06,550 --> 00:37:09,910
have a short prompt that gets processed with a very long

781
00:37:09,910 --> 00:37:11,950
prompt, this same thing goes all the way down

782
00:37:11,950 --> 00:37:13,810
to the basic attention operation.

783
00:37:13,810 --> 00:37:15,870
If you're processing a batch of inputs

784
00:37:15,870 --> 00:37:18,210
and one input is very short, one input is very long,

785
00:37:18,210 --> 00:37:22,390
you're going to be waiting for the very long input to finish.

786
00:37:22,390 --> 00:37:27,030
And then because you're running these across multiple kernels,

787
00:37:27,030 --> 00:37:29,830
you will actually start to see these gaps between kernels start

788
00:37:29,830 --> 00:37:32,270
to add up.

789
00:37:32,270 --> 00:37:35,090
So one thing that we put together to try to solve this,

790
00:37:35,090 --> 00:37:36,570
is a thing called Megakernels.

791
00:37:36,570 --> 00:37:38,950
So this is basically saying instead

792
00:37:38,950 --> 00:37:41,750
of treating each operation in the model as its own operation

793
00:37:41,750 --> 00:37:44,430
writing a kernel for it, instead, let's write a single kernel

794
00:37:44,430 --> 00:37:47,030
to cover multiple operations at once.

795
00:37:47,030 --> 00:37:49,910
This is similar to the fusion that you see in Flash Attention,

796
00:37:49,910 --> 00:37:53,910
except done more aggressively across a larger

797
00:37:53,910 --> 00:37:54,973
number of things.

798
00:37:54,973 --> 00:37:56,390
And in particular, what it does is

799
00:37:56,390 --> 00:38:00,290
it turns a GPU from a single device--

800
00:38:00,290 --> 00:38:02,990
a single operation, is you start thinking of the GPU

801
00:38:02,990 --> 00:38:05,290
as a massive distributed system and saying,

802
00:38:05,290 --> 00:38:07,650
OK, I have all this work that I need to get done.

803
00:38:07,650 --> 00:38:09,890
Some of it has dependencies on other stuff.

804
00:38:09,890 --> 00:38:12,350
So that red stuff has some dependencies

805
00:38:12,350 --> 00:38:14,390
on some of the green bars.

806
00:38:14,390 --> 00:38:15,410
How can I schedule it?

807
00:38:15,410 --> 00:38:20,850
How can I distribute the work to maximize my GPU utilization?

808
00:38:23,430 --> 00:38:26,090
If you do this just to the attention inference kernel,

809
00:38:26,090 --> 00:38:27,910
you get 30% to 70% speedups.

810
00:38:27,910 --> 00:38:29,910
And then the nice thing is that you can actually

811
00:38:29,910 --> 00:38:31,710
do this to the whole model.

812
00:38:31,710 --> 00:38:35,010
So this is one layer of a Llama-1B model.

813
00:38:35,010 --> 00:38:39,990
So here, we've basically taken the entire layer

814
00:38:39,990 --> 00:38:41,563
and put it together into one kernel.

815
00:38:41,563 --> 00:38:43,230
If you look at all these different bars,

816
00:38:43,230 --> 00:38:46,360
you see things are overlapped in really weird ways.

817
00:38:46,360 --> 00:38:49,960
That's because we are now starting to overlap a weight

818
00:38:49,960 --> 00:38:54,920
load from the next-- from the layer into the attention,

819
00:38:54,920 --> 00:38:57,960
or starting to run parts of reduction

820
00:38:57,960 --> 00:38:59,900
before the attention operation is over.

821
00:39:03,280 --> 00:39:05,660
Here's one interesting example.

822
00:39:05,660 --> 00:39:07,700
So here's one thing that you can do.

823
00:39:07,700 --> 00:39:12,300
So if you look at modern LLMs, you have the attention layer.

824
00:39:12,300 --> 00:39:14,060
You have the QKV projections.

825
00:39:14,060 --> 00:39:16,440
You're going to add some rope scaling to it.

826
00:39:16,440 --> 00:39:17,940
So that's these blue lines.

827
00:39:17,940 --> 00:39:20,840
So those blue lines are QKV plus rope.

828
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:22,360
And then the orange lines, this is

829
00:39:22,360 --> 00:39:24,660
the beginning of the attention.

830
00:39:24,660 --> 00:39:29,840
So one of the insights is that you can start loading in your KV

831
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:33,720
Cache into your attention before you're finished with QKV,

832
00:39:33,720 --> 00:39:35,680
and particularly, during decode.

833
00:39:35,680 --> 00:39:37,680
So these orange bars with some of these circles,

834
00:39:37,680 --> 00:39:41,720
you start the KV Cache load while the QKV plus rope is still

835
00:39:41,720 --> 00:39:42,360
running.

836
00:39:42,360 --> 00:39:45,670
And then once QKV is done, then you have your new query tokens.

837
00:39:45,670 --> 00:39:48,530
You can run the rest of the attention operation.

838
00:39:48,530 --> 00:39:53,510
So basically, when you have this fine-grained control of GPUs,

839
00:39:53,510 --> 00:39:58,410
you can start some operations before others are over.

840
00:39:58,410 --> 00:40:01,150
Here's another one.

841
00:40:01,150 --> 00:40:03,450
So again, the orange is the first part

842
00:40:03,450 --> 00:40:05,190
of the attention computation.

843
00:40:05,190 --> 00:40:08,610
The red is the O projection that comes after attention.

844
00:40:08,610 --> 00:40:10,070
And you have your O projection.

845
00:40:10,070 --> 00:40:12,450
Start loading the weights before your attention operation

846
00:40:12,450 --> 00:40:13,110
is over.

847
00:40:15,810 --> 00:40:21,330
We put this together in a very relatively complex CUDA

848
00:40:21,330 --> 00:40:24,410
framework with basically instruction and instruction

849
00:40:24,410 --> 00:40:26,650
based abstraction, where we can implement

850
00:40:26,650 --> 00:40:30,210
each sub-kernel in its own file, and then

851
00:40:30,210 --> 00:40:32,650
have a big virtualized shared memory

852
00:40:32,650 --> 00:40:37,070
system to orchestrate the running of these operations.

853
00:40:40,580 --> 00:40:43,140
And to do this, we put together this library

854
00:40:43,140 --> 00:40:48,420
called ThunderKittens, which is one of these kernel writing

855
00:40:48,420 --> 00:40:49,080
libraries.

856
00:40:49,080 --> 00:40:51,000
You can think of it as almost like Triton,

857
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:52,740
except more low level, a lot more

858
00:40:52,740 --> 00:40:56,820
fine-grained control over things.

859
00:40:56,820 --> 00:41:00,460
The payoff is you get near speed of light decoding inference.

860
00:41:00,460 --> 00:41:03,300
So these numbers are actually a lot better now.

861
00:41:03,300 --> 00:41:06,940
But the Megakernel shown by this bar in teal,

862
00:41:06,940 --> 00:41:09,380
and you can see it runs a lot faster than some

863
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:11,620
of the other state of the art engines.

864
00:41:11,620 --> 00:41:14,620
And on the H100, it's achieving 72%

865
00:41:14,620 --> 00:41:17,940
bandwidth utilization, which is near the speed of light

866
00:41:17,940 --> 00:41:18,720
on the GPU.

867
00:41:18,720 --> 00:41:22,220
So if you just ignore all the complexities of what we're

868
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:25,020
doing here and say, how fast can the GPU physically

869
00:41:25,020 --> 00:41:26,640
go to do this operation?

870
00:41:26,640 --> 00:41:32,380
We are pretty close to 72% of that speed of light.

871
00:41:32,380 --> 00:41:35,300
So one interesting bit there is a takeaway from that section,

872
00:41:35,300 --> 00:41:38,020
is that, if you have very deep control of the kernels

873
00:41:38,020 --> 00:41:40,460
and the understanding of the hardware,

874
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:43,900
you can start to enable very different compute paradigms.

875
00:41:43,900 --> 00:41:45,620
And all these things, you only see

876
00:41:45,620 --> 00:41:51,020
when you start playing with inference at a deep level.

877
00:41:51,020 --> 00:41:54,120
And now, I'll talk about something that's a little bit new.

878
00:41:54,120 --> 00:41:58,780
So this is now getting into the realm of new architectures.

879
00:41:58,780 --> 00:42:01,540
And I'm going to be talking about this new model called

880
00:42:01,540 --> 00:42:02,040
Parcae.

881
00:42:02,040 --> 00:42:04,180
So this is some work that comes out

882
00:42:04,180 --> 00:42:06,780
of my UCSD lab led by Hayden, and then

883
00:42:06,780 --> 00:42:12,300
also in collaboration with these two folks, Zachary and Taylor.

884
00:42:12,300 --> 00:42:13,880
So I'm going to come back to this.

885
00:42:13,880 --> 00:42:16,300
So at the beginning of the talk, I talked about, hey,

886
00:42:16,300 --> 00:42:17,877
all these new capabilities are coming

887
00:42:17,877 --> 00:42:20,460
because you're starting to scale these models, and parameters,

888
00:42:20,460 --> 00:42:21,252
and data.

889
00:42:21,252 --> 00:42:23,460
With Parcae, we wanted to ask another question, which

890
00:42:23,460 --> 00:42:26,220
is, is this the only way you have to scale?

891
00:42:26,220 --> 00:42:28,460
Or is it potentially, is there potentially something

892
00:42:28,460 --> 00:42:32,660
else, some other way that you can get this quality?

893
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:35,820
And with Parcae, we wanted to-- this was basically

894
00:42:35,820 --> 00:42:38,930
our take on a technique called loop transformers,

895
00:42:38,930 --> 00:42:41,190
where you take some blocks of your transformer

896
00:42:41,190 --> 00:42:42,570
and you run them in a loop.

897
00:42:42,570 --> 00:42:46,673
So instead of having your tokens go all one

898
00:42:46,673 --> 00:42:48,090
layer at a time through the model,

899
00:42:48,090 --> 00:42:51,010
at some point you say, hey, as you're going through,

900
00:42:51,010 --> 00:42:54,750
just send it back through that loop at a time.

901
00:42:54,750 --> 00:42:57,250
You're going to see, there's a couple different pieces here.

902
00:42:57,250 --> 00:43:00,870
So one is, we use some State Space Model theory, some SSM

903
00:43:00,870 --> 00:43:03,810
theory to stabilize this operation.

904
00:43:03,810 --> 00:43:07,110
Naively, if you just run this and let the thing train,

905
00:43:07,110 --> 00:43:09,150
we saw that the thing was going to blow up.

906
00:43:09,150 --> 00:43:10,790
And then the other thing is we started

907
00:43:10,790 --> 00:43:14,030
to see some interesting scaling laws that suggest that you want

908
00:43:14,030 --> 00:43:17,470
to be scaling the recurrence of these recurrent models

909
00:43:17,470 --> 00:43:19,790
as you increase the data.

910
00:43:19,790 --> 00:43:24,890
So in order to make the best use out of your parameters,

911
00:43:24,890 --> 00:43:29,030
you want to actually be reusing them somewhat.

912
00:43:29,030 --> 00:43:31,270
First, I'll talk a little bit of motivation

913
00:43:31,270 --> 00:43:35,120
of why we found this looping problem to be very interesting.

914
00:43:35,120 --> 00:43:39,280
So the basic idea is, let's say you have some activation that's

915
00:43:39,280 --> 00:43:41,280
going through part of your model, at some point,

916
00:43:41,280 --> 00:43:43,800
it hits these loop blocks and it's just going

917
00:43:43,800 --> 00:43:45,340
to run the same activation.

918
00:43:45,340 --> 00:43:48,460
It's going to run through that same layer some number of times.

919
00:43:48,460 --> 00:43:51,400
So that purple block is the recurrent block.

920
00:43:51,400 --> 00:43:55,440
And then at the end, you get the thing that comes back.

921
00:43:55,440 --> 00:43:57,980
所以这样做有一些优势。

922
00:43:57,980 --> 00:43:59,700
所以你可以保持参数量不变，

923
00:43:59,700 --> 00:44:02,440
但它给了你一个调节旋钮来增加你的浮点运算量。

924
00:44:02,440 --> 00:44:06,240
所以如果你认为浮点运算量——更多的浮点运算量

925
00:44:06,240 --> 00:44:08,080
等于更高的质量，那么这是一种

926
00:44:08,080 --> 00:44:12,040
在不付出更高参数成本的情况下提升质量的方法。

927
00:44:12,040 --> 00:44:15,080
另一件事是，有一项早期的工作——

928
00:44:15,080 --> 00:44:17,360
早期是个相对的说法，几年前——

929
00:44:17,360 --> 00:44:19,860
它表明你实际上能获得更高的表达力——

930
00:44:19,860 --> 00:44:21,060
它们的表达力。

931
00:44:21,060 --> 00:44:26,040
有些东西你无法表达

932
00:44:26,040 --> 00:44:27,880
在相同参数量下，

933
00:44:27,880 --> 00:44:32,560
而在这些循环模型中你可以表达。

934
00:44:32,560 --> 00:44:34,720
我们驱动性问题之一是，

935
00:44:34,720 --> 00:44:36,288
每个参数的最佳质量是什么？

936
00:44:36,288 --> 00:44:38,080
每个参数的最佳智能是什么？

937
00:44:38,080 --> 00:44:40,320
或者说每参数和每数据量的智能

938
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:44,640
这些东西能让你做到什么？

939
00:44:44,640 --> 00:44:46,820
有一些有希望的初步结果。

940
00:44:46,820 --> 00:44:51,357
这是马里兰大学Tom Goldstein组的一篇论文，

941
00:44:51,357 --> 00:44:53,440
它表明，嘿，这个东西可能比

942
00:44:53,440 --> 00:44:55,320
变换器更好。

943
00:44:55,320 --> 00:44:59,852
这些是在Arc任务上的一些结果。

944
00:44:59,852 --> 00:45:01,560
而且还有一堆Twitter上的炒作，

945
00:45:01,560 --> 00:45:04,760
因为在我们发布Parcae大约一周前，

946
00:45:04,760 --> 00:45:08,120
OpenAI的某个人说Claude mythos是一个循环的——

947
00:45:08,120 --> 00:45:09,920
是一个循环语言模型。

948
00:45:09,920 --> 00:45:11,005
我认为这不对。

949
00:45:11,005 --> 00:45:12,380
我觉得他只是在编造。

950
00:45:12,380 --> 00:45:17,640
但结果是，它真的在Twitter上炸开了

951
00:45:17,640 --> 00:45:21,122
在这之前的各种猜测中。

952
00:45:21,122 --> 00:45:23,580
最终，他不得不写这篇博客文章，像是说，嘿，

953
00:45:23,580 --> 00:45:25,080
我的错，我只是编造的。

954
00:45:25,080 --> 00:45:26,920
这些都不是真的。

955
00:45:26,920 --> 00:45:29,020
但它足够有趣，值得一看。

956
00:45:29,020 --> 00:45:31,890
所以我们在这次Twitter炒作之前就在研究它，

957
00:45:31,890 --> 00:45:35,690
但我觉得这里有一些有趣的东西，一些

958
00:45:35,690 --> 00:45:38,250
值得一看的东西。

959
00:45:38,250 --> 00:45:40,410
现在，这些循环模型的一个问题是

960
00:45:40,410 --> 00:45:43,270
如果你看其中的任何一个，并且你试着训练它们，

961
00:45:43,270 --> 00:45:46,010
and then you changed anything about the training algorithm

962
00:45:46,010 --> 00:45:46,510
at all.

963
00:45:46,510 --> 00:45:50,030
So if you change the learning rate by a little bit,

964
00:45:50,030 --> 00:45:52,350
you'd suddenly start to see these models blow up.

965
00:45:52,350 --> 00:45:54,910
So if you did a simple thing like a learning rate sweep,

966
00:45:54,910 --> 00:45:57,970
you'd see nine times out of 10, this model just

967
00:45:57,970 --> 00:46:00,030
isn't going to converge, it's going to blow up.

968
00:46:00,030 --> 00:46:01,072
You're going to get NaNs.

969
00:46:01,072 --> 00:46:03,050
You're going to get these big loss spikes.

970
00:46:03,050 --> 00:46:04,810
And so we were saying, hey, there

971
00:46:04,810 --> 00:46:06,393
seems to be something wrong with this.

972
00:46:06,393 --> 00:46:08,893
So if you're training a model and you're actually scaling it

973
00:46:08,893 --> 00:46:11,130
up, and you see there, these big loss spikes, that

974
00:46:11,130 --> 00:46:13,610
suggests something has gone very wrong with your training

975
00:46:13,610 --> 00:46:15,970
process, you should take a deeper look

976
00:46:15,970 --> 00:46:18,465
and try to figure out what happened.

977
00:46:18,465 --> 00:46:20,090
In previous work, there were some hacks

978
00:46:20,090 --> 00:46:22,010
like you can put norms in every layer

979
00:46:22,010 --> 00:46:25,250
to figure out what's happening or just pick two--

980
00:46:25,250 --> 00:46:27,190
the learning rate of 2e-4.

981
00:46:27,190 --> 00:46:29,360
Don't pick any of the other learning rates.

982
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:32,500
But we were thinking, hey, the existence of these loss spikes

983
00:46:32,500 --> 00:46:39,140
suggests that there's probably something deeper going on.

984
00:46:39,140 --> 00:46:42,300
And so we took a bit of a mathematical state space

985
00:46:42,300 --> 00:46:47,500
model-esque approach to the stabilization question.

986
00:46:47,500 --> 00:46:52,620
Our basic insight was, you can look at this process.

987
00:46:52,620 --> 00:46:54,560
So actually, if you look at this process,

988
00:46:54,560 --> 00:46:58,692
you try to think about how to analyze it analytically.

989
00:46:58,692 --> 00:47:00,900
It's going to be very complicated, because this big R

990
00:47:00,900 --> 00:47:03,260
block has tons of parameters.

991
00:47:03,260 --> 00:47:05,040
There's all sorts of nonlinearities.

992
00:47:05,040 --> 00:47:08,420
There's a Softmax, there's GELU, and RoPE,

993
00:47:08,420 --> 00:47:09,680
and all these other things.

994
00:47:09,680 --> 00:47:14,180
So if you try to analyze it analytically, it's quite complex.

995
00:47:14,180 --> 00:47:16,160
Our insight was, we were just saying,

996
00:47:16,160 --> 00:47:19,620
OK, let's actually just look at the residual of this thing.

997
00:47:19,620 --> 00:47:23,980
So how is this activation changing from block to block?

998
00:47:23,980 --> 00:47:25,758
Our first empirical observation was, hey,

999
00:47:25,758 --> 00:47:27,300
it actually doesn't change that much.

1000
00:47:27,300 --> 00:47:30,500
So each of these residual blocks is

1001
00:47:30,500 --> 00:47:32,040
changing the vector a little bit,

1002
00:47:32,040 --> 00:47:37,940
but not really having a massive impact.

1003
00:47:37,940 --> 00:47:41,100
And as a result, how can we-- so maybe

1004
00:47:41,100 --> 00:47:42,280
we can actually model this.

1005
00:47:42,280 --> 00:47:46,340
Maybe we can actually look at what's happening more deeply.

1006
00:47:46,340 --> 00:47:49,700
And so what we did is we went and wrote down a dynamic system

1007
00:47:49,700 --> 00:47:51,180
over this residual.

1008
00:47:51,180 --> 00:47:53,820
And when we did, we realized there's a couple pieces in here

1009
00:47:53,820 --> 00:47:57,660
that when you look at how you write down the model,

1010
00:47:57,660 --> 00:48:02,140
might look relatively benign, but actually

1011
00:48:02,140 --> 00:48:04,060
end up being a huge bit.

1012
00:48:04,060 --> 00:48:06,980
So what we did was we said, OK, there's all this nonlinear

1013
00:48:06,980 --> 00:48:11,060
stuff, there's this attention, there's this GELU,

1014
00:48:11,060 --> 00:48:12,700
there's this big feed-forward network

1015
00:48:12,700 --> 00:48:16,420
with the intermediates and stuff,

1016
00:48:16,420 --> 00:48:18,080
we're going to all put that into a box.

1017
00:48:18,080 --> 00:48:20,460
We're just going to call that R. And this R

1018
00:48:20,460 --> 00:48:23,020
is going to be some big nonlinear thing.

1019
00:48:23,020 --> 00:48:29,270
But we're going to put it-- just take it to the side.

1020
00:48:29,270 --> 00:48:32,850
What you're left with is these A and B matrices.

1021
00:48:32,850 --> 00:48:37,630
So this B matrix is some transformation

1022
00:48:37,630 --> 00:48:40,450
over your initial vector.

1023
00:48:40,450 --> 00:48:43,790
So what is that first vector before you start the loop?

1024
00:48:43,790 --> 00:48:47,470
And then this A vector is-- this A matrix is how do you transform

1025
00:48:47,470 --> 00:48:50,630
that residual in each loop?

1026
00:48:50,630 --> 00:48:53,670
And what we did is we said, hey, this simple thing, if you take

1027
00:48:53,670 --> 00:48:56,190
all the complexity of the transformer,

1028
00:48:56,190 --> 00:48:59,110
stick it to the side, you have a relatively simple way

1029
00:48:59,110 --> 00:49:01,750
of looking at all the previous loop transformers.

1030
00:49:01,750 --> 00:49:04,070
And in previous cases, you do end up

1031
00:49:04,070 --> 00:49:05,970
making some pretty normal decisions.

1032
00:49:05,970 --> 00:49:10,590
So in one case you just say, oh, you just

1033
00:49:10,590 --> 00:49:11,897
treat it as the identity.

1034
00:49:11,897 --> 00:49:13,230
You're just going to add things.

1035
00:49:13,230 --> 00:49:18,150
In another case, it's a fully learnable matrix.

1036
00:49:18,150 --> 00:49:21,550
And then we said, OK, what happens if we just so--

1037
00:49:21,550 --> 00:49:24,800
the residual-- we observed empirically,

1038
00:49:24,800 --> 00:49:28,400
that these A and B matrices are actually dominating

1039
00:49:28,400 --> 00:49:30,880
the magnitude of this equation.

1040
00:49:30,880 --> 00:49:34,280
What happens if you just drop that complicated nonlinear

1041
00:49:34,280 --> 00:49:34,820
piece?

1042
00:49:34,820 --> 00:49:35,820
What do you get out?

1043
00:49:35,820 --> 00:49:38,320
Well, you get out actually a pretty simple system

1044
00:49:38,320 --> 00:49:40,140
that if you use high school calculus,

1045
00:49:40,140 --> 00:49:42,000
you can just solve it to figure out what

1046
00:49:42,000 --> 00:49:44,200
the answers are going to be.

1047
00:49:44,200 --> 00:49:46,240
So here's the closed form solution.

1048
00:49:46,240 --> 00:49:48,520
You can actually compute what is,

1049
00:49:48,520 --> 00:49:50,320
given what the initial activation is,

1050
00:49:50,320 --> 00:49:52,500
and what that initial injection is,

1051
00:49:52,500 --> 00:49:54,920
you can actually just empirically compute

1052
00:49:54,920 --> 00:50:00,280
what the activation is going to look like at Step T plus 1.

1053
00:50:00,280 --> 00:50:01,940
And you notice a couple of things.

1054
00:50:01,940 --> 00:50:08,040
So this thing is dominated by these A matrices.

1055
00:50:08,040 --> 00:50:11,160
And especially, this A matrix that you are powering up

1056
00:50:11,160 --> 00:50:14,600
to a large degree.

1057
00:50:14,600 --> 00:50:17,600
And from here, what we realize is

1058
00:50:17,600 --> 00:50:20,080
that there's this quantity called the spectral radius

1059
00:50:20,080 --> 00:50:22,610
of this A. So spectral radius is basically

1060
00:50:22,610 --> 00:50:24,675
like another word for norm.

1061
00:50:24,675 --> 00:50:29,630
One way that you can look at this that, you're taking this matrix.

1062
00:50:29,630 --> 00:50:32,770
You're powering it up to huge amounts.

1063
00:50:32,770 --> 00:50:36,010
If this matrix can learn to be something like--

1064
00:50:36,010 --> 00:50:38,330
let's say that this matrix--

1065
00:50:38,330 --> 00:50:40,890
if you go to scalars, imagine that this matrix is 2.

1066
00:50:40,890 --> 00:50:43,470
And then this T is like 16 or something,

1067
00:50:43,470 --> 00:50:44,910
you've now taken this activation,

1068
00:50:44,910 --> 00:50:47,370
you've blown it up to 2 to the power of 16.

1069
00:50:47,370 --> 00:50:48,970
And it's really big.

1070
00:50:48,970 --> 00:50:52,490
And this starts to explain some of those big loss spikes.

1071
00:50:52,490 --> 00:50:55,130
And in particular, what we found is that, the choices that you

1072
00:50:55,130 --> 00:50:59,050
make for these A and B matrices that people

1073
00:50:59,050 --> 00:51:01,690
have made in these previous papers are either--

1074
00:51:01,690 --> 00:51:05,370
we call them, marginally stable or unstable.

1075
00:51:05,370 --> 00:51:08,390
Unstable-- make the system unstable.

1076
00:51:10,970 --> 00:51:13,090
And so with Parcae we said, OK, well, we

1077
00:51:13,090 --> 00:51:16,410
figured out this thing where if you take this particular look

1078
00:51:16,410 --> 00:51:19,992
at it, and you let these A and B do whatever they want,

1079
00:51:19,992 --> 00:51:21,950
you're going to get things that really explode.

1080
00:51:21,950 --> 00:51:27,290
What if we just constrain A and B such that if you run the math they're not going to explode?

1081
00:51:27,290 --> 00:51:29,950
So for A, what we did is we said,

1082
00:51:29,950 --> 00:51:31,970
OK, we're effectively going to make

1083
00:51:31,970 --> 00:51:34,190
that A matrix a negative diagonal matrix.

1084
00:51:34,190 --> 00:51:37,910
So if you power that up, the term eventually goes to zero,

1085
00:51:37,910 --> 00:51:39,675
so it doesn't blow up.

1086
00:51:39,675 --> 00:51:41,050
This B matrix, we're going to put

1087
00:51:41,050 --> 00:51:43,730
a-- stick a really simple linear norm against it,

1088
00:51:43,730 --> 00:51:46,330
because this B matrix actually only gets applied once

1089
00:51:46,330 --> 00:51:48,570
and doesn't really blow up.

1090
00:51:48,570 --> 00:51:49,930
E can be the spectral radius.

1091
00:51:49,930 --> 00:51:51,305
It's now going to be less than 1.

1092
00:51:51,305 --> 00:51:55,130
It's now actually going to be a stable system.

1093
00:51:55,130 --> 00:51:57,930
And now if you go train this model, what we saw

1094
00:51:57,930 --> 00:52:00,270
is that you actually saw a stable loss curve.

1095
00:52:00,270 --> 00:52:03,870
So Parcae is this stabilized thing where we re-parameterize

1096
00:52:03,870 --> 00:52:08,570
A and B. You can see that even with this the 6e-4 learning rate

1097
00:52:08,570 --> 00:52:11,030
that was so bad for the other models,

1098
00:52:11,030 --> 00:52:13,890
you actually got a stable model at the end.

1099
00:52:13,890 --> 00:52:16,490
And you see that with doing this,

1100
00:52:16,490 --> 00:52:22,420
you can naturally constrain the state norm of the activations.

1101
00:52:22,420 --> 00:52:23,560
So two pieces here.

1102
00:52:23,560 --> 00:52:26,260
So that orange baseline is just a completely unconstrained

1103
00:52:26,260 --> 00:52:26,760
model.

1104
00:52:26,760 --> 00:52:29,700
You can see it blows up, goes to 10 to the 19th.

1105
00:52:29,700 --> 00:52:34,740
This blue line is actually a model where

1106
00:52:34,740 --> 00:52:37,100
you are applying a norm to it.

1107
00:52:37,100 --> 00:52:39,860
So what happens here is that the model is actually

1108
00:52:39,860 --> 00:52:44,000
trying to expand the activations because it's saying,

1109
00:52:44,000 --> 00:52:48,240
oh, with more room, I can represent different things better.

1110
00:52:48,240 --> 00:52:50,540
I can put these different concepts further away

1111
00:52:50,540 --> 00:52:52,482
from each other or whatnot.

1112
00:52:52,482 --> 00:52:53,940
And then you're applying norm to it

1113
00:52:53,940 --> 00:52:56,120
to take that big thing that's trying to expand.

1114
00:52:56,120 --> 00:52:59,140
Then you try to norm it back down to one.

1115
00:52:59,140 --> 00:53:01,180
And then you have this big--

1116
00:53:01,180 --> 00:53:04,220
these two pressures that are fighting against each other that

1117
00:53:04,220 --> 00:53:05,640
manifests in loss spikes.

1118
00:53:05,640 --> 00:53:08,160
So even though on the right, your norms are very good,

1119
00:53:08,160 --> 00:53:10,820
you're not seeing the activation actually blow up.

1120
00:53:10,820 --> 00:53:16,230
You do see that the loss can do some pretty gnarly things.

1121
00:53:16,230 --> 00:53:19,350
So basically, there's this pretty simple change

1122
00:53:19,350 --> 00:53:22,610
in the activations can stabilize the training,

1123
00:53:22,610 --> 00:53:26,590
stabilize these recurrent loops.

1124
00:53:26,590 --> 00:53:28,370
So it's not only a more stable system,

1125
00:53:28,370 --> 00:53:31,550
you actually also get higher quality models.

1126
00:53:31,550 --> 00:53:36,710
So this is a table where we are comparing Parcae models

1127
00:53:36,710 --> 00:53:39,790
against a previous loop transformer called Recurrent

1128
00:53:39,790 --> 00:53:40,490
Depth Model.

1129
00:53:40,490 --> 00:53:42,190
So you can see higher performance

1130
00:53:42,190 --> 00:53:45,430
across a variety of applications.

1131
00:53:45,430 --> 00:53:48,790
So you see Parcae outperforms the previous models

1132
00:53:48,790 --> 00:53:51,250
and also outperforms strong transformer baselines.

1133
00:53:51,250 --> 00:53:54,550
So this transformer is one of the nanochat ones where

1134
00:53:54,550 --> 00:53:56,590
a bunch of people are trying to just get

1135
00:53:56,590 --> 00:53:58,870
it to learn as fast as it can.

1136
00:53:58,870 --> 00:54:01,550
And these Parcae models, if you take that same basic transformer

1137
00:54:01,550 --> 00:54:04,890
architecture, start looping it, and then stabilize it,

1138
00:54:04,890 --> 00:54:08,950
you get better perplexities, better end

1139
00:54:08,950 --> 00:54:10,490
to end quality as well.

1140
00:54:13,030 --> 00:54:14,790
And then one-- and then we started

1141
00:54:14,790 --> 00:54:17,830
to run some very basic scaling laws here.

1142
00:54:17,830 --> 00:54:20,790
And here, our question was, OK, this is really, really cute.

1143
00:54:20,790 --> 00:54:23,210
You can do this looping thing to make things better.

1144
00:54:23,210 --> 00:54:25,510
But really, should you-- is there any evidence

1145
00:54:25,510 --> 00:54:27,150
that there's something here that we

1146
00:54:27,150 --> 00:54:32,590
want to be doing looping more aggressively?

1147
00:54:32,590 --> 00:54:34,710
So here, I'll take a step back.

1148
00:54:34,710 --> 00:54:37,310
A few years ago, there was all this work

1149
00:54:37,310 --> 00:54:41,030
as we started to enter this regime where you wanted

1150
00:54:41,030 --> 00:54:43,590
to scale up all these models, a natural question

1151
00:54:43,590 --> 00:54:45,470
is, should I make the models bigger

1152
00:54:45,470 --> 00:54:47,950
or should I just train on more data?

1153
00:54:47,950 --> 00:54:49,790
So a few years ago, folks started

1154
00:54:49,790 --> 00:54:53,037
to ask, how should I scale these two quantities with each other?

1155
00:54:53,037 --> 00:54:54,370
Should I scale model parameters?

1156
00:54:54,370 --> 00:54:55,910
Should I scale data?

1157
00:54:55,910 --> 00:54:59,750
And we came up with all these very complicated power law

1158
00:54:59,750 --> 00:55:01,497
curves that all look like this.

1159
00:55:01,497 --> 00:55:02,330
They're very pretty.

1160
00:55:02,330 --> 00:55:03,570
There's lots of colors.

1161
00:55:03,570 --> 00:55:07,910
What you want to look out for is basically--

1162
00:55:07,910 --> 00:55:10,790
what you want to look out for is, if that curve is going down

1163
00:55:10,790 --> 00:55:12,400
into the right, it suggests you should

1164
00:55:12,400 --> 00:55:16,140
be scaling both data and parameters at the same time.

1165
00:55:16,140 --> 00:55:17,640
If we were going straight down, that

1166
00:55:17,640 --> 00:55:20,620
would mean just increase your training data.

1167
00:55:20,620 --> 00:55:23,958
There's no need to increase your parameters.

1168
00:55:23,958 --> 00:55:26,000
If it's going-- if it's flat, just going straight

1169
00:55:26,000 --> 00:55:29,660
to the right, that means don't increase your data at all,

1170
00:55:29,660 --> 00:55:31,347
just increase your parameters.

1171
00:55:31,347 --> 00:55:33,180
But you see, it's going down into the right.

1172
00:55:33,180 --> 00:55:36,385
That means you should scale data and parameters at the same time.

1173
00:55:36,385 --> 00:55:37,260
And now you see this.

1174
00:55:37,260 --> 00:55:38,840
You train a $1 trillion parameter

1175
00:55:38,840 --> 00:55:44,280
model on $35 trillion tokens, and you get better quality.

1176
00:55:44,280 --> 00:55:48,200
So our question was, OK, where does recurrence fit into this?

1177
00:55:48,200 --> 00:55:50,180
And there are a couple--

1178
00:55:50,180 --> 00:55:52,980
so down into the right means you want to scale data and params--

1179
00:55:52,980 --> 00:55:57,760
there are a couple possibilities with recurrence.

1180
00:55:57,760 --> 00:56:00,640
And so for example, you might conclude,

1181
00:56:00,640 --> 00:56:02,260
you should never run a recurrence.

1182
00:56:02,260 --> 00:56:05,380
It's better to just keep the same single recurrent model.

1183
00:56:05,380 --> 00:56:07,860
You might conclude, you should do a ton of recurrence,

1184
00:56:07,860 --> 00:56:12,970
or maybe only some recurrence to the bit.

1185
00:56:12,970 --> 00:56:16,250
What we're showing here is, at least in these initial scaling

1186
00:56:16,250 --> 00:56:20,010
laws, all of these curves are isometric,

1187
00:56:20,010 --> 00:56:21,630
and ISO-param, ISO-FLOP.

1188
00:56:21,630 --> 00:56:23,955
So each of these curves is a model.

1189
00:56:23,955 --> 00:56:25,330
So on the left and the right, you

1190
00:56:25,330 --> 00:56:27,650
have the same number of parameters.

1191
00:56:27,650 --> 00:56:29,828
As you go down, as you change the colors,

1192
00:56:29,828 --> 00:56:31,370
we are increasing the amount of flops

1193
00:56:31,370 --> 00:56:34,570
that you use to train the model by increasing

1194
00:56:34,570 --> 00:56:35,730
the amount of data.

1195
00:56:35,730 --> 00:56:38,290
So here, we're varying data and varying

1196
00:56:38,290 --> 00:56:40,650
the number of recurrences.

1197
00:56:40,650 --> 00:56:43,010
What we find is that in both of these models,

1198
00:56:43,010 --> 00:56:45,130
you see this down into the right trend again.

1199
00:56:45,130 --> 00:56:49,290
So what this is suggesting is that for these fixed parameter

1200
00:56:49,290 --> 00:56:52,150
training things, as you increase the amount of data,

1201
00:56:52,150 --> 00:56:54,770
you should actually also be increasing the amount

1202
00:56:54,770 --> 00:57:00,290
of recurrences that you have.

1203
00:57:00,290 --> 00:57:03,690
We find that these recurrences follow some pretty classic power

1204
00:57:03,690 --> 00:57:04,810
laws.

1205
00:57:04,810 --> 00:57:07,650
So you can actually start to predict.

1206
00:57:07,650 --> 00:57:11,130
You can get these scaling laws to start to predict quality,

1207
00:57:11,130 --> 00:57:15,230
as you scale, recurrences and your tokens jointly.

1208
00:57:15,730 --> 00:57:18,650
For a fixed model size [INAUDIBLE] recurrence.

1209
00:57:18,650 --> 00:57:21,110
What about jointly [INAUDIBLE] scaling [INAUDIBLE]?

1210
00:57:21,610 --> 00:57:26,730
So we have this really complex 3D figure that showed

1211
00:57:26,730 --> 00:57:30,610
recurrences, and data, and parameters.

1212
00:57:30,610 --> 00:57:35,650
And it pointed also whatever down into the right,

1213
00:57:35,650 --> 00:57:39,250
and down that way at the same time.

1214
00:57:39,250 --> 00:57:41,970
So if you believe that figure, it

1215
00:57:41,970 --> 00:57:44,930
suggests you should be scaling all three together.

1216
00:57:44,930 --> 00:57:47,810
But that figure was just really hard to look at, because it was

1217
00:57:47,810 --> 00:57:50,430
3D and weird.

1218
00:57:50,430 --> 00:57:54,630
So these power laws suggest, when you're increasing data

1219
00:57:54,630 --> 00:57:56,930
you should be increasing recurrence.

1220
00:57:56,930 --> 00:57:59,423
And then you have other power laws that suggest,

1221
00:57:59,423 --> 00:58:02,090
when you're increasing data you should be increasing parameters.

1222
00:58:02,090 --> 00:58:04,853
So obviously, you should increase--

1223
00:58:04,853 --> 00:58:07,020
well, it suggests that you should increase all three

1224
00:58:07,020 --> 00:58:09,100
of them if you can.

1225
00:58:09,100 --> 00:58:11,740
But one piece here is that if you're

1226
00:58:11,740 --> 00:58:13,380
going to fix your model size and you're

1227
00:58:13,380 --> 00:58:15,180
going to increase the amount of data,

1228
00:58:15,180 --> 00:58:18,340
you should also be increasing the recurrence, which

1229
00:58:18,340 --> 00:58:21,580
is interesting because as far as I know, all of our models

1230
00:58:21,580 --> 00:58:23,080
today have no recurrence in them.

1231
00:58:23,080 --> 00:58:26,980
So they're all at the very left of these curves.

1232
00:58:26,980 --> 00:58:28,770
And they all have a ton of data, which

1233
00:58:28,770 --> 00:58:31,020
suggests that there might be something slightly better

1234
00:58:31,020 --> 00:58:36,180
that we could be doing when training these models.

1235
00:58:36,180 --> 00:58:40,240
And here's just that experiment in another way.

1236
00:58:40,240 --> 00:58:43,940
So on the orange curve, you have-- so here,

1237
00:58:43,940 --> 00:58:46,680
we fixed the model size.

1238
00:58:46,680 --> 00:58:49,920
The orange curve is a fixed depth model.

1239
00:58:49,920 --> 00:58:52,200
So this is like a traditional transformer model.

1240
00:58:52,200 --> 00:58:57,900
The blue curve is when you fix the FLOP budget,

1241
00:58:57,900 --> 00:59:02,420
where do you hit on the curve for a looping model?

1242
00:59:02,420 --> 00:59:06,170
So here, the orange and blue dots

1243
00:59:06,170 --> 00:59:08,170
have been trained with the same number of FLOPs,

1244
00:59:08,170 --> 00:59:11,590
but a different amount of data, but they're the same size.

1245
00:59:11,590 --> 00:59:13,830
When you get to that number of FLOPs

1246
00:59:13,830 --> 00:59:16,990
by increasing recurrences as well as--

1247
00:59:16,990 --> 00:59:18,470
and not just increasing data, you

1248
00:59:18,470 --> 00:59:22,830
start to get smaller validation losses, which again suggests

1249
00:59:22,830 --> 00:59:26,270
it might be the case that we should be looping all

1250
00:59:26,270 --> 00:59:27,770
of our big pre-training runs.

1251
00:59:30,910 --> 00:59:32,630
So that's Parcae.

1252
00:59:32,630 --> 00:59:36,550
If I take a little step back and go back

1253
00:59:36,550 --> 00:59:40,110
to the big takeaway of the talk, I think hopefully,

1254
00:59:40,110 --> 00:59:41,910
I've given you a little sense today

1255
00:59:41,910 --> 00:59:44,450
of if you understand the inference of these models,

1256
00:59:44,450 --> 00:59:46,350
if you understand the GPU kernels,

1257
00:59:46,350 --> 00:59:50,290
you understand all the pieces that goes into it.

1258
00:59:50,290 --> 00:59:53,077
You can really start to enable full stack innovation

1259
00:59:53,077 --> 00:59:54,410
and machine learning algorithms.

1260
00:59:54,410 --> 00:59:57,110
So whether that's through a new routing algorithm that

1261
00:59:57,110 --> 01:00:00,970
lets you serve more traffic, serve traffic in a different way.

1262
01:00:00,970 --> 01:00:03,960
Or new kernels that allow you to run part of your system way

1263
01:00:03,960 --> 01:00:04,740
faster.

1264
01:00:04,740 --> 01:00:07,800
Or new architectures that say, if you

1265
01:00:07,800 --> 01:00:10,120
have a lot fewer parameters, you can fit them

1266
01:00:10,120 --> 01:00:12,740
in a subset of your GPUs in a different way,

1267
01:00:12,740 --> 01:00:15,120
reduce the amount of communication,

1268
01:00:15,120 --> 01:00:18,600
these are all different pieces of that question

1269
01:00:18,600 --> 01:00:23,520
of that research problem that we're seeing today.

1270
01:00:23,520 --> 01:00:26,520
And yeah, hopefully I've inspired at least one of you

1271
01:00:26,520 --> 01:00:31,120
in this room to go take a deeper look at some of that.

1272
01:00:31,120 --> 01:00:32,340
So with that, thanks.

1273
01:00:32,340 --> 01:00:33,820
Happy to take a few questions.

1274
01:00:33,820 --> 01:00:35,775
[APPLAUSE]

1275
01:00:37,720 --> 01:00:41,100
We have about five, 10 minutes for questions.

1276
01:00:41,100 --> 01:00:43,020
So if you have a question, raise your hand.

1277
01:00:46,000 --> 01:00:50,460
Parcae, are you training from scratch on the loop?

1278
01:00:50,460 --> 01:00:51,960
I guess I was wondering if you could

1279
01:00:51,960 --> 01:00:54,360
拍一张[INAUDIBLE]的照片。

1280
01:00:56,720 --> 01:00:57,220
是的。

1281
01:00:57,220 --> 01:00:57,845
好问题。

1282
01:00:57,845 --> 01:01:00,080
所以问题是，用Parcae，

1283
01:01:00,080 --> 01:01:01,640
我们是从头训练吗？

1284
01:01:01,640 --> 01:01:04,560
有没有什么可以用预训练模型做的事情？

1285
01:01:04,560 --> 01:01:08,010
所以几个月前有一个人发了一篇很钓鱼的博客文章，

1286
01:01:08,010 --> 01:01:09,760
大概就是说，嘿，我

1287
01:01:09,760 --> 01:01:13,740
没有训练任何东西就赢了一个排行榜比赛。

1288
01:01:13,740 --> 01:01:17,160
他做的是，他实际上循环了Quinn模型中的两三层，

1289
01:01:17,160 --> 01:01:20,880
然后只是

1290
01:01:20,880 --> 01:01:25,240
发现它在一些数学任务上开始有更高的质量。

1291
01:01:25,240 --> 01:01:27,080
所以我们有一些工作正在

1292
01:01:27,080 --> 01:01:29,600
研究这个，可能很快就会出来。

1293
01:01:29,600 --> 01:01:31,400
但可能有一些模型，

1294
01:01:31,400 --> 01:01:33,520
如果你对预训练模型做一点点循环，

1295
01:01:33,520 --> 01:01:37,200
你就能得到更高质量的东西，

1296
01:01:37,200 --> 01:01:38,260
这真的很奇怪。

1297
01:01:38,260 --> 01:01:39,620
这让我感到不安。

1298
01:01:39,620 --> 01:01:42,880
我不知道为什么会这样。

1299
01:01:42,880 --> 01:01:45,860
但是的，我们会——我们对此非常感兴趣。

1300
01:01:45,860 --> 01:01:48,800
我想我们会研究它，希望，

1301
01:01:48,800 --> 01:01:50,480
如果我能说服Hayden，他会

1302
01:01:50,480 --> 01:01:54,640
盯着激活——盯着实际的权重

1303
01:01:54,640 --> 01:01:58,450
去弄清楚为什么当你循环它时，它会变得更好。

1304
01:01:58,450 --> 01:01:59,950
这里就这个问题追问一下。

1305
01:01:59,950 --> 01:02:03,670
所以你谈到了循环模型的计算最优性，

1306
01:02:03,670 --> 01:02:06,050
你能谈谈推理方面的影响

1307
01:02:06,050 --> 01:02:09,590
以及内存，以及它如何能让你跑得更快吗？

1308
01:02:09,590 --> 01:02:10,090
是的。

1309
01:02:10,090 --> 01:02:11,882
所以其中一个让我个人

1310
01:02:11,882 --> 01:02:14,010
对这些循环东西非常兴奋的原因

1311
01:02:14,010 --> 01:02:16,702
是，服务推理的一个大瓶颈

1312
01:02:16,702 --> 01:02:18,410
实际上

1313
01:02:18,410 --> 01:02:20,010
最终是GPU内存。

1314
01:02:20,010 --> 01:02:22,050
所以如果你有更少的参数量，你

1315
01:02:22,050 --> 01:02:24,930
可以装下，比如，更多的KV Cache，

1316
01:02:24,930 --> 01:02:27,930
或者你可以做更少的通信，因为你

1317
01:02:27,930 --> 01:02:31,390
需要把你的模型分布到更少的GPU上，诸如此类。

1318
01:02:31,390 --> 01:02:33,730
所以实际上有很多灵活性

1319
01:02:33,730 --> 01:02:36,810
That a smaller model will get you.

1320
01:02:36,810 --> 01:02:38,370
I also had this dream that if you

1321
01:02:38,370 --> 01:02:41,130
could make the recurrent block small enough,

1322
01:02:41,130 --> 01:02:42,970
you could actually write a little megakernel

1323
01:02:42,970 --> 01:02:45,450
to just do that recurrent in a little very

1324
01:02:45,450 --> 01:02:47,405
fast megakernel loop.

1325
01:02:47,405 --> 01:02:50,030
So far, we haven't been able to make those blocks small enough.

1326
01:02:50,030 --> 01:02:52,930
But I think it's quite interesting.

1327
01:02:52,930 --> 01:02:55,470
Certainly, with the next generation of the LPU,

1328
01:02:55,470 --> 01:02:57,960
the Groq chips that are coming with NVIDIA things.

1329
01:02:57,960 --> 01:03:02,120
Those have 250 megabytes of memory or something like that.

1330
01:03:02,120 --> 01:03:04,340
So the thing that you'll be able to fit into them

1331
01:03:04,340 --> 01:03:05,532
is very, very small.

1332
01:03:05,532 --> 01:03:07,740
But maybe you can design something that will actually

1333
01:03:07,740 --> 01:03:11,500
fit into them, and then you can just keep your weights in memory

1334
01:03:11,500 --> 01:03:13,740
the whole time and just run your activations through

1335
01:03:13,740 --> 01:03:14,800
as quickly as you can.

1336
01:03:14,800 --> 01:03:17,100
So there are non-linear benefits that you

1337
01:03:17,100 --> 01:03:20,300
can get if you can cross some of these thresholds

1338
01:03:20,300 --> 01:03:22,920
that I'm hoping that we'll be able to get to pretty soon.

1339
01:03:25,940 --> 01:03:27,140
Yeah, OK.

1340
01:03:27,140 --> 01:03:30,560
[INAUDIBLE] I'm curious.

1341
01:03:30,560 --> 01:03:33,980
I guess the trade-offs are that it's always

1342
01:03:33,980 --> 01:03:35,660
strictly more optimal.

1343
01:03:35,660 --> 01:03:37,780
[INAUDIBLE] Great.

1344
01:03:37,780 --> 01:03:40,620
So the question is, what are the trade-offs for my kernels?

1345
01:03:40,620 --> 01:03:44,020
The trade-offs are people's blood, sweat, and tears.

1346
01:03:44,020 --> 01:03:46,780
So megakernels, turns out they're very, very labor

1347
01:03:46,780 --> 01:03:48,580
intensive to write.

1348
01:03:48,580 --> 01:03:57,540
So to give you some context, a full talented kernel engineer

1349
01:03:57,540 --> 01:03:59,460
over the course of a year, will probably

1350
01:03:59,460 --> 01:04:03,190
be able to write megakernels for one hardware for two

1351
01:04:03,190 --> 01:04:06,900
or three models for batch sizes 1 to 16.

1352
01:04:06,900 --> 01:04:09,900
Go batch size 17, he was like nope, start over.

1353
01:04:09,900 --> 01:04:11,288
Got to go again.

1354
01:04:11,288 --> 01:04:13,080
So they're very, very challenging to write.

1355
01:04:13,080 --> 01:04:14,830
And together, we're trying to put together

1356
01:04:14,830 --> 01:04:17,940
some compilers that can automate some of that process.

1357
01:04:17,940 --> 01:04:21,800
But I'd say, it's a very challenging thing to do.

1358
01:04:21,800 --> 01:04:24,140
I think the basic megakernel idea

1359
01:04:24,140 --> 01:04:29,780
has gone in peaks and troughs over the last few decades

1360
01:04:29,780 --> 01:04:31,940
when it comes to GPU programming.

1361
01:04:31,940 --> 01:04:34,680
But yeah, so if you can do it, it will go super fast.

1362
01:04:34,680 --> 01:04:37,380
You will never be able to go faster,

1363
01:04:37,380 --> 01:04:41,000
but it just takes a lot of energy and a lot of effort.

1364
01:04:44,500 --> 01:04:45,860
Cool.

1365
01:04:45,860 --> 01:04:47,380
Maybe I can ask a question.

1366
01:04:47,380 --> 01:04:50,860
So can we talk a little bit more about co-design?

1367
01:04:50,860 --> 01:04:54,750
And in particular, you mentioned all these new hardware

1368
01:04:54,750 --> 01:04:57,190
like Grok and Cerebras on the inference side.

1369
01:04:57,190 --> 01:05:00,110
So if you're designing a model and that's

1370
01:05:00,110 --> 01:05:01,570
going to be the serving platform,

1371
01:05:01,570 --> 01:05:03,690
how should you be changing your architecture?

1372
01:05:03,690 --> 01:05:06,530
Yeah, I think there's a couple of things

1373
01:05:06,530 --> 01:05:07,530
that you should look at.

1374
01:05:07,530 --> 01:05:11,310
So one is, you are going to be most constrained by memory

1375
01:05:11,310 --> 01:05:11,990
first.

1376
01:05:11,990 --> 01:05:14,510
So if you know that you're going to be taking a model

1377
01:05:14,510 --> 01:05:19,160
and serving it a particular Cerebras chip,

1378
01:05:19,160 --> 01:05:20,910
you want to go look at the Cerebras wafer,

1379
01:05:20,910 --> 01:05:23,190
and figure out how much memory you have, and then

1380
01:05:23,190 --> 01:05:26,390
size your model so that it can fit there with enough KV cache

1381
01:05:26,390 --> 01:05:29,310
or whatever to spare.

1382
01:05:29,310 --> 01:05:31,750
If you look at carefully, at the Chinese models that

1383
01:05:31,750 --> 01:05:35,510
are coming out, they've started making some interesting choices

1384
01:05:35,510 --> 01:05:37,710
that suggest they might be starting

1385
01:05:37,710 --> 01:05:41,110
to think about the Huawei chips that are coming out.

1386
01:05:41,110 --> 01:05:44,690
You'll see quantization choices that people make.

1387
01:05:44,690 --> 01:05:46,350
So if you have a model that you're

1388
01:05:46,350 --> 01:05:48,530
intending to serve on NVIDIA GPUs,

1389
01:05:48,530 --> 01:05:51,040
for example, NVIDIA's new Nemotron model that they

1390
01:05:51,040 --> 01:05:54,420
released, you will train that model in NVFP4.

1391
01:05:54,420 --> 01:05:58,400
This is a FP4 format that is proprietary to NVIDIA chips.

1392
01:05:58,400 --> 01:06:01,780
If you're not going to run it on NVIDIA chips, if you're AMD,

1393
01:06:01,780 --> 01:06:04,960
then you're going to run this other format called MXFP4.

1394
01:06:04,960 --> 01:06:08,360
They each have their pros and cons.

1395
01:06:08,360 --> 01:06:11,360
You'll make these subtle choices based on the hardware

1396
01:06:11,360 --> 01:06:14,120
that you're choosing.

1397
01:06:14,120 --> 01:06:17,360
I think there was a question over there.

1398
01:06:17,360 --> 01:06:20,160
I was wondering if it's ever-- if you just care about

1399
01:06:20,160 --> 01:06:22,720
compute optimal training, is it ever optimal to loop

1400
01:06:22,720 --> 01:06:24,920
as opposed to just adding more parameters,

1401
01:06:24,920 --> 01:06:28,920
or is it mainly like a trick to reduce inference cost?

1402
01:06:28,920 --> 01:06:30,040
Yeah.

1403
01:06:30,040 --> 01:06:31,600
So the trick with compute optimal

1404
01:06:31,600 --> 01:06:35,220
is it is, usually, like given a flop budget--repeat the question.

1405
01:06:35,220 --> 01:06:35,520
Oh yeah.

1406
01:06:35,520 --> 01:06:36,200
Sorry.

1407
01:06:36,200 --> 01:06:38,040
Yeah, the question was for Parcae,

1408
01:06:38,040 --> 01:06:39,600
would you ever choose to loop instead

1409
01:06:39,600 --> 01:06:41,880
of increasing parameters?

1410
01:06:41,880 --> 01:06:43,920
And so the trick with compute optimal

1411
01:06:43,920 --> 01:06:46,100
is always given some flop budget,

1412
01:06:46,100 --> 01:06:48,880
figure out what you want to hit.

1413
01:06:48,880 --> 01:06:51,260
It's almost a little bit contrived in that sense,

1414
01:06:51,260 --> 01:06:54,180
because if you want a higher quality model,

1415
01:06:54,180 --> 01:06:56,100
you should just increase your FLOPs budget.

1416
01:06:56,100 --> 01:06:59,400
And if you decided on your model size, just train longer.

1417
01:06:59,400 --> 01:07:04,680
Or if you're restricted by your model size, then loop longer.

1418
01:07:04,680 --> 01:07:07,960
Or if you run out of data, then pick the model size

1419
01:07:07,960 --> 01:07:11,560
that you think will be as overtrained-- as

1420
01:07:11,560 --> 01:07:14,520
well trained as you can for that size.

1421
01:07:14,520 --> 01:07:17,200
So I think there are choices.

1422
01:07:17,200 --> 01:07:20,480
And of course, you make all these choices in the context of,

1423
01:07:20,480 --> 01:07:22,780
do I think it'll get picked up?

1424
01:07:22,780 --> 01:07:24,348
How am I going to serve it?

1425
01:07:24,348 --> 01:07:26,140
If you're going to release the open source,

1426
01:07:26,140 --> 01:07:29,480
what is the size of model that people can serve

1427
01:07:29,480 --> 01:07:31,120
like on your laptop today?

1428
01:07:31,120 --> 01:07:34,180
So I think all these choices go into making a choice.

1429
01:07:34,180 --> 01:07:37,280
All these things go into making a choice of how big a model

1430
01:07:37,280 --> 01:07:39,612
you choose to train.

1431
01:07:39,612 --> 01:07:41,320
I think if you just make the model bigger

1432
01:07:41,320 --> 01:07:44,520
and train it on more data, it's always going to get better.

1433
01:07:44,520 --> 01:07:51,230
But I think it really depends on those design points.

1434
01:07:54,970 --> 01:07:56,955
Maybe another question about co-design.

1435
01:07:56,955 --> 01:07:58,330
So you mentioned at the beginning

1436
01:07:58,330 --> 01:08:03,290
different use cases like agentic coding versus let's say batch

1437
01:08:03,290 --> 01:08:05,150
processing of data.

1438
01:08:05,150 --> 01:08:09,530
So what are the most dramatic differences

1439
01:08:09,530 --> 01:08:12,050
in terms of optimal architectures

1440
01:08:12,050 --> 01:08:14,470
that you see across different use cases?

1441
01:08:14,470 --> 01:08:16,770
And at the end of the day, of course

1442
01:08:16,770 --> 01:08:20,569
model developers has to pick one and try to not be

1443
01:08:20,569 --> 01:08:23,010
reasonable on many use cases.

1444
01:08:23,010 --> 01:08:24,010
Yeah.

1445
01:08:24,010 --> 01:08:25,109
That's a great question.

1446
01:08:25,109 --> 01:08:28,790
So I think one of the big differences--

1447
01:08:28,790 --> 01:08:31,750
so when you have these agentic looped workflows,

1448
01:08:31,750 --> 01:08:33,930
one of the things that matters a lot

1449
01:08:33,930 --> 01:08:37,590
is that you want to keep your KV cache as hot as possible.

1450
01:08:37,590 --> 01:08:40,210
So if you're doing a big batch processing thing where you only

1451
01:08:40,210 --> 01:08:42,990
see each document once and then you translate it,

1452
01:08:42,990 --> 01:08:47,220
the KV cache doesn't necessarily matter as much.

1453
01:08:47,220 --> 01:08:50,240
If you look at something like the DeepSeek MLA attention,

1454
01:08:50,240 --> 01:08:52,700
that is a radical compression of the KV

1455
01:08:52,700 --> 01:08:56,899
cache compared to what you would have for another model.

1456
01:08:56,899 --> 01:09:01,420
Or if you have the model that can process its KV cache in FP8

1457
01:09:01,420 --> 01:09:04,140
or FP4, those are pretty big departures

1458
01:09:04,140 --> 01:09:06,500
in terms of the size of the KV cache

1459
01:09:06,500 --> 01:09:08,939
that if you are sensitive to an agentic workflow

1460
01:09:08,939 --> 01:09:10,859
you would look at.

1461
01:09:10,859 --> 01:09:13,740
Then of course, the biggest one is like causal attention

1462
01:09:13,740 --> 01:09:15,700
or non-causal attention.

1463
01:09:15,700 --> 01:09:18,365
So if you're just doing a big batch processing workflows.

1464
01:09:18,365 --> 01:09:21,939
Like for the longest time, Google was just using BERT models.

1465
01:09:21,939 --> 01:09:25,580
I think probably still uses BERT models on search.

1466
01:09:25,580 --> 01:09:27,385
And that's because you don't really

1467
01:09:27,385 --> 01:09:29,760
need to be generating a bunch of tokens on the other end.

1468
01:09:29,760 --> 01:09:33,460
So you just do that big bidirectional attention once,

1469
01:09:33,460 --> 01:09:35,140
you get your vector out, then you

1470
01:09:35,140 --> 01:09:38,380
stick that in a database, do whatever you will with it.

1471
01:09:38,380 --> 01:09:43,040
Whereas the chat workflows, those processing,

1472
01:09:43,040 --> 01:09:46,437
there's always going to be this decode portion of it.

1473
01:09:46,437 --> 01:09:48,229
I think there have been intermediate things

1474
01:09:48,229 --> 01:09:53,510
like T5 was at some point a choice that people made

1475
01:09:53,510 --> 01:09:59,710
to do some bidirectional processing, and then also some generation.

1476
01:09:59,710 --> 01:10:03,510
OK, maybe one last question.

1477
01:10:03,510 --> 01:10:04,210
Oh, yeah.

1478
01:10:04,210 --> 01:10:09,510
[INAUDIBLE], it was about using everything

1479
01:10:09,510 --> 01:10:12,710
into one kernel, [INAUDIBLE] but what

1480
01:10:12,710 --> 01:10:15,190
you were talking about before.

1481
01:10:15,190 --> 01:10:18,950
So I was just wondering, how does that [INAUDIBLE]

1482
01:10:18,950 --> 01:10:21,870
work a communication across multiple computers

1483
01:10:21,870 --> 01:10:26,270
are scaling the cost of [INAUDIBLE] that.

1484
01:10:26,270 --> 01:10:27,170
Yeah, great question.

1485
01:10:27,170 --> 01:10:29,510
So the question is about, how does Megakernel

1486
01:10:29,510 --> 01:10:34,590
work when you have multiple GPUs communication in the loop?

1487
01:10:34,590 --> 01:10:38,010
We had some very early preliminary work about this.

1488
01:10:38,010 --> 01:10:40,670
So it turns out you can also fuse the NCCL

1489
01:10:40,670 --> 01:10:44,270
calls into the Megakernel if you set it up correctly.

1490
01:10:44,270 --> 01:10:46,710
I think we haven't found a really great killer use

1491
01:10:46,710 --> 01:10:50,350
case for that yet, where sometimes you're

1492
01:10:50,350 --> 01:10:53,190
just bound by the latency of the NCCL call itself.

1493
01:10:53,190 --> 01:10:57,070
So these are also pieces of things that you can fuse.

1494
01:10:57,070 --> 01:11:00,790
When DeepSeek 4 came out, the DeepSeek 4s released

1495
01:11:00,790 --> 01:11:04,130
a Megakernel for the mixture of experts inference layer that can

1496
01:11:04,130 --> 01:11:06,630
run just for that where they actually did fuse some of those

1497
01:11:06,630 --> 01:11:08,230
communication.

1498
01:11:08,230 --> 01:11:10,090
So I think what we are starting to see,

1499
01:11:10,090 --> 01:11:12,670
we're starting to see more of is,

1500
01:11:12,670 --> 01:11:14,250
as you get to more of these models,

1501
01:11:14,250 --> 01:11:18,830
you'll have a little Megakernel for a part for the computation,

1502
01:11:18,830 --> 01:11:22,252
but you won't necessarily have a Megakernel for the whole model,

1503
01:11:22,252 --> 01:11:24,710
unless of course, you pay the blood, sweat, and tears price

1504
01:11:24,710 --> 01:11:28,790
and then really, really get the whole thing going.

1505
01:11:28,790 --> 01:11:30,570
OK, I think that's all the time we have.

1506
01:11:30,570 --> 01:11:31,810
